window.NUR258_QBANK={"1": [{"q": "Four clients arrive in the eye clinic. Which does the nurse see first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A client with gradual painless loss of side vision over 2 years", "A client with sudden severe eye pain, halos, vomiting, a fixed dilated pupil and a hazy cornea", "A client with a clouded lens and worsening night glare", "A client with wavy lines on an Amsler grid at home"], "ans": [1], "why": "Acute closed-angle glaucoma presents as fixed dilated pupil, hazy cornea and pain with vomiting, and it is a <b>surgical emergency</b> - pressure must come down or vision is lost. The gradual painless peripheral loss is chronic open-angle glaucoma: serious, but managed with lifelong drops, not tonight."}, {"q": "A client with labyrinthitis has vomited 4 times in 3 hours and is dizzy. Which assessment is the priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Fluid and electrolyte status", "Effectiveness of the vestibular suppressant", "Tympanic membrane appearance", "Ability to ambulate to the bathroom"], "ans": [0], "why": "Once vomiting is repetitive the problem stops being vestibular and becomes volume: <b>fluid and electrolyte assessment is the priority</b>. Reaching for meclizine treats the dizziness the client came in with, not the dehydration she has developed since."}, {"q": "A client is prescribed two different ophthalmic drops in the same eye. Which instruction is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Instill both drops back to back, then blink several times", "Wait 5 to 10 minutes between the two drops and press the inner canthus for 2 minutes", "Wait 30 seconds between drops and wipe the eye from outer to inner canthus", "Wait 30 minutes between drops and keep the eye closed for 10 minutes"], "ans": [1], "why": "Different drops need <b>5 to 10 minutes</b> apart so the first is not washed out, and occluding the inner canthus for <b>2 minutes</b> blocks systemic absorption through the nasolacrimal duct. Back-to-back instillation simply flushes the first drug away."}, {"q": "Which findings are risk factors for glaucoma? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Long-term corticosteroid use", "Wearing corrective lenses", "Thin central cornea", "Daily vitamin A supplement", "High myopia"], "ans": [0, 2, 4], "why": "The risk list is family history, age over 60, diabetes, hypertension, <b>corticosteroid use, thin central cornea and high myopia</b>. Corrective lenses, vitamin A and sunglasses were named explicitly as non-risks - refraction and nutrition do not raise intraocular pressure."}, {"q": "A client is receiving IV gentamicin along with scheduled furosemide. What is the nurse's priority action?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Increase oral fluid intake to protect the kidneys", "Monitor for early hearing loss and report it so the dose can be adjusted", "Teach the client to avoid loud environments during therapy", "Request that the furosemide be given at bedtime"], "ans": [1], "why": "Aminoglycoside plus loop diuretic is the ototoxic pairing to memorize, and the priority is to <b>detect early hearing loss and report it so the dose can change</b>. Pushing fluids and avoiding noise sound protective but do nothing about drug-induced cochlear damage."}, {"q": "A client reports a painless curtain moving across her visual field that started this morning. A new nurse offers her a routine appointment next week. How should the nurse respond?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Agree, since painless vision changes are not urgent", "Arrange immediate evaluation; retinal detachment is an emergency despite being painless", "Instruct her to patch the eye and rest at home for 24 hours", "Tell her this is expected with cataract formation"], "ans": [1], "why": "A painless curtain across the vision is <b>retinal detachment - an emergency</b>, and it is the one eye condition that may require face-down (prone) positioning for about 2 weeks post-op. The trap is equating absence of pain with absence of urgency."}, {"type": "sata", "week": "1", "step": "action", "topic": "Sensory Disorders - Eye & Ear", "q": "A client with Meniere's disease is having an acute attack of vertigo with nausea and nystagmus. Which actions should the nurse take right now? (Select all that apply.)", "opts": ["Dim the lights and lower the noise level in the room.", "Raise the side rails and keep the client in bed until the attack passes.", "Walk the client to the bathroom now so the client does not become incontinent.", "Give the prescribed meclizine and antiemetic.", "Offer strong coffee to help the client stay alert.", "Coach the client to turn the head slowly and avoid sudden position changes."], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 5], "why": "During an attack the nurse controls the environment and prevents falls, so dim quiet surroundings, raised rails, bed rest, and slow head movement are the immediate actions. Meclizine settles the vestibular symptoms and an antiemetic treats the vomiting. Walking a client who has active vertigo and nystagmus is how falls happen, so elimination needs are met at the bedside. Caffeine is a trigger that worsens inner-ear symptoms rather than helping.", "src": "ATI ch 14 Middle and Inner Ear Disorders", "icon": "👁️", "topicFull": "Sensory Disorders - Eye & Ear", "pharm": 1}, {"q": "A client is scheduled for cataract surgery on the right eye. Which statement by the client tells the nurse the teaching was understood?", "type": "single", "opts": ["\"I will bend at the waist to pick things up so I do not strain my back.\"", "\"I will avoid lifting anything heavier than about 10 pounds and will not strain on the toilet.\"", "\"I should expect sharp pain in the eye for the first two days.\"", "\"I can sleep on my right side as long as I use a soft pillow.\""], "ans": [1], "why": "Everything after cataract surgery is about <b>keeping intraocular pressure down</b>: no heavy lifting, no straining, no bending at the waist, and sleeping on the <b>unaffected</b> side. Bending and side-lying on the operative eye both raise pressure. <b>Sharp pain is never expected</b> - it suggests hemorrhage or increased pressure and must be reported at once.", "topic": "Sensory Disorders - Eye & Ear", "icon": "👁️", "topicFull": "Sensory Disorders - Eye & Ear"}, {"q": "A nurse instils two different eye drops for the same eye. Which actions are correct? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Wait at least 5 minutes between the two drops", "Place the drop in the conjunctival sac, not on the cornea", "Apply punctal occlusion for 30 to 60 seconds after a beta blocker drop", "Let the dropper tip rest on the lower lash to steady the hand", "Give the suspension (milky) drop before the clear solution"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "Spacing the drops stops the second one washing the first away, and the <b>conjunctival sac</b> is the target because the cornea is exquisitely sensitive. <b>Punctal occlusion</b> matters most with timolol and the other beta blockers - it keeps the drug out of the systemic circulation, where it can drop the heart rate and trigger bronchospasm. Touching the dropper to the lash contaminates it. The order is reversed: <b>solutions first, suspensions last</b>.", "topic": "Sensory Disorders - Eye & Ear", "icon": "👁️", "topicFull": "Sensory Disorders - Eye & Ear"}, {"q": "An older client with presbycusis is admitted. Which nursing actions support communication? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Face the client and make sure your mouth is visible", "Lower the pitch of your voice", "Shout to be sure you are heard", "Reduce background noise before starting", "Rephrase rather than simply repeating a sentence that was not understood"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 4], "why": "Presbycusis takes the <b>high frequencies first</b>, so lowering pitch helps and shouting does not - shouting raises pitch and distorts the face, destroying the lip-reading cues the client is relying on. Facing the client, cutting background noise and rephrasing all give the brain a second route to the meaning.", "topic": "Sensory Disorders - Eye & Ear", "icon": "👁️", "topicFull": "Sensory Disorders - Eye & Ear"}, {"q": "A client reports vertigo, tinnitus, and a sense of fullness in one ear, with episodes lasting hours. Which instruction is the priority for safety?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Increase dietary sodium to maintain fluid balance", "Sit or lie down immediately when an attack begins and do not drive", "Perform vigorous head-turning exercises during an attack", "Irrigate the ear canal with warm water twice daily"], "ans": [1], "why": "Ménière's disease brings on vertigo without warning; the danger is <b>falling or crashing a car</b>, so the client stops moving and does not drive. Sodium is <b>restricted</b>, not increased, because it worsens endolymph volume. Head movement makes an attack worse, and irrigation has no role.", "topic": "Sensory Disorders - Eye & Ear", "icon": "👁️", "topicFull": "Sensory Disorders - Eye & Ear"}, {"q": "Which findings in a client with open-angle glaucoma should the nurse expect? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Gradual loss of peripheral vision", "Sudden severe eye pain with vomiting", "Intraocular pressure above 21 mm Hg", "Increased cup-to-disc ratio on examination", "Central vision loss with straight lines appearing wavy"], "ans": [0, 2, 3], "why": "Open-angle glaucoma is the <b>silent</b> one: pressure creeps up, the optic disc cups, and <b>peripheral</b> vision goes first, which is why it is often found on a routine exam. Sudden pain with vomiting is <b>closed-angle</b> glaucoma, an emergency. Central loss with wavy lines is <b>macular degeneration</b>.", "topic": "Sensory Disorders - Eye & Ear", "icon": "👁️", "topicFull": "Sensory Disorders - Eye & Ear"}, {"q": "A client with a history of closed-angle glaucoma is admitted for hip surgery. The pre-op orders include scopolamine for nausea. What is the nurse's priority action?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Hold the dose and contact the prescriber before it is given", "Give the dose and recheck the pupils in one hour", "Give the dose along with the client's beta-blocker eye drops", "Hold the dose only if the client begins to report eye pain"], "ans": [0], "why": "Scopolamine is an <b>anticholinergic</b>, and anticholinergics dilate the pupil, which jams the drainage angle shut and spikes the pressure, so they are contraindicated in closed-angle glaucoma. Waiting for eye pain is tempting but that pain <b>is</b> the acute attack, and the drops do not protect against a drug that is already dilating the pupil."}, {"q": "One day after surgical repair of a detached retina with a gas bubble, a client is found lying flat on his back watching television. What should the nurse do first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Help him back into the face-down position and review that it continues about 2 weeks", "Raise the head of the bed to 30 degrees and let him rest", "Turn him onto the operative side to keep pressure off the other eye", "Apply a warm compress to the eye and notify the surgeon"], "ans": [0], "why": "Retinal detachment repair is the <b>one</b> eye surgery that gets face-down (prone) positioning, for roughly 2 weeks, so the gas bubble presses the retina back into place. Raising the head of the bed feels like a safe, comfortable compromise, but any position other than face-down lets the bubble float off the retina; warm compresses and operative-side positioning are not used."}, {"q": "A client with macular degeneration calls the clinic and reports that the straight lines on her home Amsler grid look wavy today, though her side vision is unchanged. Which response by the nurse is best?", "type": "single", "opts": ["\"Come in to be evaluated - new wavy lines can mean wet macular degeneration, which is treated with anti-VEGF injections.\"", "\"That is expected as central vision fades; keep checking the grid and call in a month.\"", "\"Stop using the grid; it only screens for rising eye pressure.\"", "\"Your peripheral vision is intact, so no follow-up is needed right now.\""], "ans": [0], "why": "The whole point of the home Amsler grid is that <b>wavy lines</b> catch a change early, and wet macular degeneration is treated with anti-VEGF injections. Calling the change expected is tempting because macular degeneration does take central vision, but intact peripheral vision is the normal pattern of this disease and is not a reason to delay evaluation."}, {"q": "A client struck on the side of the head reports severe ear pain that suddenly stopped, followed by drainage and muffled hearing on that side. Which instruction is most important?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Keep the ear dry and avoid swimming and flying until it has healed", "Irrigate the ear canal daily to flush out the drainage", "Expect that surgery will be scheduled to close the opening", "The pain stopping means the injury resolved, so no follow-up is needed"], "ans": [0], "why": "Pain that <b>suddenly relieves</b> is the classic sign of a ruptured tympanic membrane, and the care is to keep the ear dry with no swimming or flying while it heals on its own. Assuming the pain stopping means resolution is exactly backwards - the relief marks the rupture - and routine surgery is not the expected treatment."}, {"q": "A client with type 2 diabetes for 8 years tells the nurse he cancelled his eye appointment because his vision seems fine. Which teaching points should the nurse include? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["A dilated eye exam is needed every year even when vision seems normal", "Keeping the A1C at target is the real protection for the retinas", "Controlling blood pressure also protects vision", "An eye exam is only needed once blurred vision develops", "Daily eye drops are prescribed to prevent diabetic retinopathy"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "Every diabetic gets an <b>annual dilated exam regardless of symptoms</b>, and the real prevention is tight A1C and blood pressure control. Waiting for blurred vision is the trap the client already fell into, and daily drops belong to glaucoma, not to preventing diabetic retinopathy."}, {"q": "A 78-year-old client is admitted after finishing a long course of IV gentamicin. He worked 30 years in a factory without hearing protection and also has impacted cerumen and a history of otosclerosis. Which factors put him at risk for permanent hearing loss? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["The prolonged course of gentamicin", "Thirty years of loud occupational noise", "His age of 78", "The impacted cerumen in the canal", "The history of otosclerosis"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "Sensorineural loss damages the hair cells or nerve and is <b>usually permanent</b>; its causes are noise, age, and ototoxic drugs such as aminoglycosides like gentamicin. Cerumen and otosclerosis are tempting because they clearly affect hearing, but they are <b>conductive</b> problems - sound cannot get in - and conductive loss is often fixable."}], "2": [{"q": "A nurse sustains a needlestick from a client who is HIV positive. What does the nurse do first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Report the exposure to the charge nurse", "Wash the site with soap and water", "Draw a baseline HIV antibody test", "Begin post-exposure prophylaxis"], "ans": [1], "why": "The sequence taught is <b>wash with soap and water first</b>, then report, start PEP and draw baseline labs. Rushing to PEP feels more urgent, but the first act reduces the inoculum still on the skin."}, {"q": "Which action most effectively prevents both CLABSI and CAUTI?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Use full sterile technique at insertion", "Do not place the device unless it is truly necessary", "Change the dressing every 7 days", "Prefer the femoral site for central access"], "ans": [1], "why": "The single most effective action is <b>not placing the device at all unless it is necessary</b>; sterile insertion is the runner-up. Femoral lines infect far more than subclavian, so that option is wrong twice over."}, {"q": "A client is to receive acetaminophen 650 mg from a 160 mg/5 mL suspension, measured at home in teaspoons. How many teaspoons should the nurse teach?", "type": "single", "opts": ["2 teaspoons", "4 teaspoons", "10 teaspoons", "20 teaspoons"], "ans": [1], "why": "650 divided by 160, times 5 mL, equals 20.3 mL; at <b>5 mL per teaspoon that is about 4 teaspoons</b>. The classic miss is stopping at 20 and calling it 20 teaspoons - the units, not the math, are the trap."}, {"q": "Which measures belong in neutropenic precautions? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Private room with positive-pressure airflow", "N95 respirator for everyone entering", "Remove fresh flowers and potted plants from the room", "Dedicated equipment kept in the room", "Keep the door open so staff can observe the client"], "ans": [0, 2, 3], "why": "Neutropenic (reverse) isolation means a <b>positive-pressure private room, no fresh flowers or plants, dedicated equipment</b>, limited visitors and daily cleaning. N95 and negative pressure belong to airborne precautions, which protect everyone else from the client - the opposite direction of risk."}, {"q": "A client is admitted with varicella. Which precautions does the nurse initiate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Contact precautions with a private room", "Airborne precautions: negative-pressure room, door closed, N95", "Droplet precautions with a surgical mask within 3 feet", "Standard precautions only until lesions crust"], "ans": [1], "why": "Varicella is one of the four airborne conditions along with TB, measles and disseminated herpes zoster, so it needs a <b>negative-pressure room, closed door and N95</b>. The skin lesions tempt students toward contact precautions, but the virus produces respiratory symptoms and spreads through the air."}, {"q": "A nurse is exposed to HIV and asks about prophylaxis timing. Which response is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Start within 72 hours at the latest and continue for 28 days", "Start within 7 days and continue for 14 days", "Start only if the baseline test is positive, then continue for 6 weeks", "Start immediately and continue until the viral load is undetectable"], "ans": [0], "why": "PEP is started as soon as possible, ideally within hours, and <b>never beyond 72 hours</b>, then continued a full <b>28 days</b>. Waiting for a baseline result wastes the window - the baseline draw documents pre-exposure status, it does not gate treatment."}, {"type": "sata", "week": "2", "step": "solutions", "topic": "Infectious Diseases & HIV", "q": "A nurse is teaching a client who has HIV about ways to reduce the risk of opportunistic infection. Which statements by the client indicate a correct understanding of the teaching? (Select all that apply.)", "opts": ["“I will wash my hands frequently throughout the day.”", "“I will avoid changing my cat's litter box.”", "“I will stop my antiretroviral therapy once my viral load is undetectable.”", "“I will keep my influenza and pneumococcal vaccinations up to date.”", "“I will eat soft-cooked eggs and rare beef for extra protein.”", "“I will avoid crowds and people who are currently sick.”"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 5], "why": "Frequent hand hygiene, avoiding litter box duty (toxoplasmosis exposure), staying current on immunizations, and avoiding crowds and sick contacts all lower infection risk for a client with HIV. Antiretroviral therapy must be continued exactly as prescribed and never stopped, even when the viral load becomes undetectable, because stopping allows the virus to rebound and increases drug-resistance risk. Undercooked eggs and rare meat carry a foodborne-illness risk that an immunocompromised client should avoid, not seek out.", "src": "ATI Adult Med-Surg ch 86 (HIV/AIDS)", "icon": "🦠", "topicFull": "Infectious Diseases & HIV"}, {"q": "A client with HIV has a CD4 count of 180 cells/mm³. Which prophylaxis does the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia", "Isoniazid for latent tuberculosis regardless of skin test result", "Azithromycin for Mycobacterium avium complex", "No prophylaxis is needed until the CD4 count is below 50"], "ans": [0], "why": "The thresholds are worth memorizing: <b>PCP prophylaxis at CD4 under 200</b>, MAC prophylaxis at <b>under 50</b>. At 180 the client needs trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole. Isoniazid is only for a positive screen, not everyone.", "pharm": 1, "topic": "Infectious Diseases & HIV", "icon": "🦠", "topicFull": "Infectious Diseases & HIV"}, {"q": "Which statements about HIV transmission are accurate? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["A client with a sustained undetectable viral load does not transmit HIV sexually", "HIV is transmitted by sharing drinking glasses", "Breast milk can transmit HIV", "Standard precautions are used for all clients regardless of HIV status", "A negative antibody test three weeks after exposure rules out infection"], "ans": [0, 2, 3], "why": "<b>Undetectable equals untransmittable</b> for sexual transmission - a central teaching point and a powerful reason for adherence. Breast milk does transmit. Standard precautions apply to everyone, which is exactly why they are called standard. HIV is <b>not</b> spread by casual contact, and a test at three weeks is inside the window period, so it rules out nothing.", "topic": "Infectious Diseases & HIV", "icon": "🦠", "topicFull": "Infectious Diseases & HIV"}, {"q": "A nurse is caring for a client on airborne precautions for tuberculosis. Which actions are correct? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Place the client in a negative-pressure room", "Wear a fit-tested N95 respirator to enter", "Have the client wear a surgical mask when transported out of the room", "Wear a surgical mask instead of a respirator if the client is masked", "Keep the room door closed"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "Airborne precautions are <b>negative pressure, door closed, N95 for staff</b>. The direction of the masks is the part people reverse: <b>staff wear the respirator, the client wears a surgical mask</b> when they must leave the room. A surgical mask does not protect the nurse from droplet nuclei.", "topic": "Infectious Diseases & HIV", "icon": "🦠", "topicFull": "Infectious Diseases & HIV", "card": "NG-364_tuberculosis-pharmacology.html", "cardLabel": "Tuberculosis — Pharmacology"}, {"q": "A client taking rifampin for tuberculosis reports orange-red urine. What is the nurse's best response?", "type": "single", "opts": ["\"Stop the medication and come in to be seen today.\"", "\"This is expected with rifampin. It also stains soft contact lenses permanently.\"", "\"That suggests kidney damage; we will check your creatinine.\"", "\"Drink more water and the color will clear within a day.\""], "ans": [1], "why": "Orange-red discoloration of urine, sweat and tears is a <b>harmless, expected</b> rifampin effect - but it <b>permanently stains soft contact lenses</b>, which is the part clients are never told. What does need reporting on rifampin is hepatotoxicity: jaundice, dark urine with pale stools, right upper quadrant pain.", "pharm": 1, "topic": "Infectious Diseases & HIV", "icon": "🦠", "topicFull": "Infectious Diseases & HIV", "card": "NG-364_tuberculosis-pharmacology.html", "cardLabel": "Tuberculosis — Pharmacology"}, {"q": "A client admitted with Clostridioides difficile has had profuse watery diarrhea for two days. Which problem does the nurse address first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Dehydration", "Electrolyte imbalance", "Perianal skin breakdown", "Poor nutritional intake"], "ans": [0], "why": "In C. diff the priority problem is <b>dehydration</b>, and electrolytes come second. Electrolyte imbalance is tempting because it comes from the same fluid loss, but it is ranked after dehydration, and hydration is assessed before antibiotics are given."}, {"q": "A nurse is caring for a mechanically ventilated client and is reviewing the unit's ventilator-associated pneumonia prevention measures. Which intervention is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Elevate the head of the bed at least 30 degrees", "Suction the airway every 2 hours on a set schedule", "Keep the client flat while the endotracheal tube is in place", "Limit oral care to once every 24 hours"], "ans": [0], "why": "VAP prevention is <b>head of bed at least 30 degrees</b>. Scheduled <b>q2h suctioning</b> is the classic wrong answer — it looks proactive, but routine suctioning traumatizes the trachea rather than preventing pneumonia."}, {"q": "A client is admitted with fever and cough attributed to an emerging infectious disease whose route of transmission has not yet been confirmed. What does the nurse do first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Place the client in the most protective isolation available", "Notify the public health department before isolating the client", "Maintain standard precautions until the route is identified", "Start droplet precautions and leave the door open"], "ans": [0], "why": "Until the route is confirmed, isolate at the <b>most protective level available</b>, then report to public health. Notifying public health first is tempting because reporting is required, but it protects no one while the client sits unisolated with an unknown route."}, {"q": "A client with HIV has been taking antiretroviral therapy for three months and asks how the team will know the medication is working. Which laboratory value does the nurse identify?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Viral load", "White blood cell count", "Absolute neutrophil count", "CD4 count"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Viral load</b> is what tracks how well ART is working, and therapy lowers it. CD4 is the tempting choice because it is the monitoring lab for HIV itself and rises with treatment, but response to therapy is judged by viral load; WBC is not the HIV monitoring lab at all."}, {"q": "A client is admitted with Clostridioides difficile after a recent course of antibiotics. Which actions does the nurse take? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Perform hand hygiene with soap and water rather than an alcohol-based rub", "Clean the room and equipment with a bleach solution", "Initiate contact precautions in a private room", "Place the client in a negative-pressure room with the door closed", "Use an alcohol-based hand rub after removing gloves"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "C. diff calls for <b>soap and water</b>, <b>bleach</b> cleaning, and <b>contact precautions</b> in a private room, because alcohol gel does not kill C. diff spores. A negative-pressure room with the door closed belongs to airborne precautions (TB, measles, varicella); with contact precautions the door may stay open."}, {"q": "A nurse is caring for a client with a subclavian central line. Which actions reduce the client's risk of a central line-associated bloodstream infection? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Scrub the hub for 20 seconds before accessing the line", "Allow chlorhexidine 2 minutes to work at dressing changes", "Change the dressing every 7 days", "Avoid drawing blood from the central line because it causes CLABSI", "Request a femoral site instead of a subclavian site"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "Scrubbing the hub <b>20 seconds</b>, giving chlorhexidine <b>2 minutes</b> at dressing changes, and changing dressings <b>q7 days</b> are the line-care rules, along with pulling the line the moment it is no longer needed. Drawing blood from the line does not itself cause CLABSI — <b>bad technique</b> does — and femoral lines infect far more than subclavian."}], "3": [{"q": "Ten minutes into an IV infusion a client reports itching and dizziness. BP is 86/52, HR 118, RR 24. What does the nurse do first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Stop the infusion", "Obtain a set of vital signs and recheck in 5 minutes", "Administer the prescribed antihistamine", "Place the client in Trendelenburg"], "ans": [0], "why": "This is type I (IgE-mediated) hypersensitivity, and the first action is to <b>stop the offending infusion</b> before any drug is given. Reaching for diphenhydramine while the antigen keeps running lets the reaction progress to anaphylaxis."}, {"q": "A client's chart lists a penicillin allergy. What does the nurse do first before the prescribed antibiotic is given?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Hold the dose and document the allergy", "Ask the client to describe exactly what happened when she took penicillin", "Notify the provider that the client is allergic", "Give the dose slowly with epinephrine at the bedside"], "ans": [1], "why": "First <b>clarify what actually happened</b> - many reported allergies are GI upset, not immune reactions. If the answer is rash, itching or swelling, then notify the provider before giving the drug."}, {"q": "A client is admitted with an acute gout flare of the great toe. Which new prescription should the nurse question?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Naproxen", "Colchicine", "Allopurinol", "Prednisone"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Allopurinol is long-term prevention and can worsen an acute flare</b>, so starting it now should be questioned. Colchicine and NSAIDs plus corticosteroids treat the attack - reversing prevention and acute treatment is the classic miss."}, {"q": "Which findings support a type I hypersensitivity reaction? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Blood pressure 86/52", "Temperature 37.3 C", "Heart rate 118", "Generalized itching", "Oxygen saturation 96 percent on room air"], "ans": [0, 2, 3], "why": "The cues scored as relevant were <b>hypotension, tachycardia, itching, dizziness, diarrhea and RR 24</b>, and about 90 percent of anaphylaxis includes urticaria or rash. Temp 37.3 and SpO2 96 percent are inside normal limits and were scored as not indicative - normal values are distractors."}, {"q": "A client is to receive diphenhydramine 25 mg from a 12.5 mg/5 mL elixir at home. How much should the nurse teach her to take?", "type": "single", "opts": ["1 teaspoon", "2 teaspoons", "4 teaspoons", "10 teaspoons"], "ans": [1], "why": "25 mg divided by 12.5 mg, times 5 mL, is 10 mL, and at 5 mL per teaspoon that is <b>2 teaspoons</b>. Answering 10 means the student stopped at millilitres and never converted."}, {"q": "A client has widespread pain, fatigue and poor sleep. ESR, CRP and ANA are all normal. Which condition does this pattern support?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Rheumatoid arthritis", "Systemic lupus erythematosus", "Fibromyalgia", "Serum sickness"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Fibromyalgia is not inflammatory, so ESR, CRP and ANA are normal</b> - that is the fastest way to separate it from RA and lupus, which show abnormal labs. Management is low-impact exercise, sleep hygiene and duloxetine, pregabalin or milnacipran, not opioids."}, {"q": "A client is having an anaphylactic reaction. Which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Administer intramuscular epinephrine", "Start an IV of normal saline", "Administer IV diphenhydramine", "Obtain a set of vital signs"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Epinephrine first, intramuscular, into the vastus lateralis</b> - it is the only drug that reverses the airway and vascular collapse. Antihistamines and steroids are adjuncts that work far too slowly to save the airway, and stopping to take vital signs delays the one thing that matters.", "pharm": 1, "topic": "Allergic, Inflammatory…", "icon": "🦠", "topicFull": "Allergic, Inflammatory & Immunologic Disorders"}, {"q": "Which findings suggest a systemic lupus erythematosus flare rather than stable disease? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["New proteinuria", "Butterfly rash worsening after a beach day", "Weight loss with fever", "Blood pressure 118/74 mm Hg", "New joint pain and swelling"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "A flare shows as <b>new organ involvement</b> - proteinuria means lupus nephritis, the complication that decides prognosis - plus fever, weight loss, worsening rash and arthritis. <b>Sun exposure is a classic trigger</b>, which is why sunscreen is a daily prescription, not a comfort measure. A normal blood pressure is reassuring, not a flare sign.", "topic": "Allergic, Inflammatory…", "icon": "🦠", "topicFull": "Allergic, Inflammatory & Immunologic Disorders"}, {"q": "A client with rheumatoid arthritis asks how it differs from osteoarthritis. Which features belong to rheumatoid arthritis? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Symmetric joint involvement", "Morning stiffness lasting more than an hour", "Fatigue, low-grade fever and weight loss", "Pain that worsens through the day with use", "Heberden's nodes at the distal finger joints"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "Rheumatoid arthritis is a <b>systemic autoimmune</b> disease: symmetric, prolonged morning stiffness, and whole-body symptoms. Osteoarthritis is <b>mechanical wear</b>: asymmetric, worse with use through the day, brief stiffness, and <b>Heberden's nodes</b> at the distal joints. Getting this pair straight answers a large number of exam items.", "topic": "Allergic, Inflammatory…", "icon": "🦠", "topicFull": "Allergic, Inflammatory & Immunologic Disorders", "card": "musc/NG-163_rheumatoid-arthritis.html", "cardLabel": "Rheumatoid Arthritis"}, {"q": "A client starting methotrexate for rheumatoid arthritis needs teaching. Which instructions are correct? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Take folic acid as prescribed", "Avoid alcohol", "Report sore throat, mouth ulcers or unusual bruising", "Take the dose every day at bedtime", "Use reliable contraception"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "Methotrexate for arthritis is <b>weekly, not daily</b> - a daily dose is a well-documented fatal medication error. Folic acid reduces the side effects, alcohol compounds the liver toxicity, sore throat and ulcers signal bone-marrow suppression, and the drug is <b>teratogenic</b>.", "pharm": 1, "topic": "Allergic, Inflammatory…", "icon": "🦠", "topicFull": "Allergic, Inflammatory & Immunologic Disorders", "card": "musc/NG-163_rheumatoid-arthritis.html", "cardLabel": "Rheumatoid Arthritis"}, {"q": "A client admitted with widespread hives that appeared shortly after a meal tells the nurse it is \"just a rash.\" Which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Inspect the lips, tongue and airway", "Apply a cool compress to the hives", "Give the ordered oral antihistamine", "Document the size and location of the hives"], "ans": [0], "why": "Urticaria can be the <b>opening act of anaphylaxis</b>, so the lips, tongue and airway are assessed before the rash is called minor. The ordered antihistamine is the tempting choice, but antihistamines never rescue an airway and giving it first delays finding swelling."}, {"q": "A client with systemic lupus erythematosus who takes hydroxychloroquine calls the clinic to report new blurred vision. Which response by the nurse is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Keep taking it and mention the change at your next routine visit", "Take each dose with food to settle the visual side effect", "Stop the drug and call the provider today, because vision changes can mean retinal damage", "This is expected during a flare and will clear when the flare settles"], "ans": [2], "why": "Any visual change on hydroxychloroquine may be <b>irreversible retinal toxicity</b>, so the drug is stopped and the provider notified; routine eye exams are required. Waiting for the next visit is tempting because the symptom sounds mild, but retinal damage allowed to progress does not reverse."}, {"q": "A client with systemic lupus erythematosus is seen for a follow-up visit. Which finding does the nurse report to the provider first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Butterfly rash across the cheeks and nose", "Rising BUN and creatinine with BP 160/98 and dark frothy urine", "Fatigue and joint pain between flares", "Skin that reddens quickly after an hour of gardening"], "ans": [1], "why": "Rising BUN and creatinine with hypertension and dark frothy urine point to <b>lupus nephritis</b>, the complication that determines survival. The butterfly rash is the tempting pick because it looks dramatic, but it is an expected finding in lupus and calls for sun-protection teaching, not an urgent report."}, {"q": "A client admitted with Raynaud's phenomenon is being taught how to limit attacks. Which statement shows correct understanding?", "type": "single", "opts": ["My fingers will turn red first, then blue, then white", "Heat brings these attacks on, so I will avoid hot water", "I will wear gloves in cold places, including the freezer aisle at the store", "When an attack starts I should run cold water over my hands"], "ans": [2], "why": "Cold triggers the vasospasm, so gloves belong in any cold environment, including the freezer aisle. The color-sequence option is tempting because the three colors are right, but the order is reversed: it is <b>white, then blue, then red</b>."}, {"q": "A client is discharged on allopurinol after repeated gout attacks. Which instructions does the nurse include? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Drink at least 2 liters of fluid a day", "Avoid organ meats and seafood", "Avoid alcohol", "Your kidney function will be monitored while you take this drug", "Start this drug at the first twinge of an acute attack to shorten it"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 3], "why": "Allopurinol is the <b>long-term</b> gout drug: push 2 or more liters of fluid a day, monitor the kidneys, and avoid organ meats, seafood and alcohol. Using it to treat an acute attack is the classic miss — it can worsen the flare, which is treated with colchicine or naproxen plus corticosteroids."}, {"q": "A client admitted for surgery reports a latex allergy. Which statements does the nurse include in teaching? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Latex-free supplies will be used for your whole stay, not only in the operating room", "Tell us if bananas, avocados, kiwi or chestnuts have ever made you itch or swell", "Report itching, or any swelling of your lips or tongue, right away", "These precautions can stop once you are back in your room after surgery", "An antihistamine before contact makes latex exposure safe for you"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "Latex allergy calls for a <b>latex-free environment for the entire stay</b>, and latex cross-reacts with banana, avocado, kiwi and chestnut, so those foods are worth asking about. Limiting precautions to the procedure is the tempting error, and premedicating does not make exposure safe — antihistamines never rescue an airway."}], "4": [{"q": "A client with burns to the face and anterior neck is hoarse and has singed nasal hair and carbonaceous sputum. What is the priority action?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Calculate the TBSA using the rule of nines", "Obtain intubation equipment now", "Start lactated Ringer's by the Parkland formula", "Apply silver sulfadiazine to the facial burns"], "ans": [1], "why": "Inhalation cues mean the <b>airway will swell shut within 1 to 2 hours</b>, so intubation equipment comes before TBSA estimation or fluids. Face, neck or chest burns make airway the priority; only waist-down burns put fluids first."}, {"q": "A client with 30 percent TBSA burns arrives in the ED. Which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Administer IV morphine for severe pain", "Insert two large-bore 18-gauge IV catheters", "Remove all clothing and jewelry", "Apply sterile dressings to the wounds"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Two large-bore (18 g) IVs come first</b> - without access there is no resuscitation and no analgesia. Pain is loud and tempting, but the line precedes the drug."}, {"q": "A 75 kg client has 54 percent TBSA partial- and full-thickness burns. Using the Parkland formula, what is the infusion rate for the first 8 hours?", "type": "single", "opts": ["506 mL/hr", "675 mL/hr", "1,013 mL/hr", "2,025 mL/hr"], "ans": [2], "why": "4 mL x 75 kg x 54 = 16,200 mL over 24 hours; half (8,100 mL) runs in the first 8 hours = <b>about 1,013 mL/hr</b> of lactated Ringer's. Halving 16,200 over 24 hours instead gives 675 - the error is forgetting the front-loaded first 8 hours, timed from injury, not arrival."}, {"q": "Which systemic effects does the nurse expect in the emergent phase of a major burn? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Hyperkalemia", "Metabolic alkalosis", "Paralytic ileus", "Hypoglycemia", "Hypothermia"], "ans": [0, 2, 4], "why": "Cell death and massive fluid shifts produce <b>hyperkalemia, hyponatremia, paralytic ileus, decreased cardiac output, hypothermia and Curling's ulcer</b>. Increased cardiac output, metabolic alkalosis and hypoglycemia were all scored wrong."}, {"q": "A client has superficial (first-degree) burns over both shoulders plus deep partial-thickness burns of the anterior trunk. Which areas are included in the Parkland calculation?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Both areas, since all burned skin loses fluid", "Only the anterior trunk", "Only the shoulders", "Neither, until TBSA exceeds 20 percent"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>First-degree burns are never counted in TBSA or in Parkland</b> - only second- and third-degree burns are. Counting the red, dry, blister-free areas inflates the volume and over-resuscitates the client."}, {"q": "A client has a white, leathery, painless burn on the thigh surrounded by moist, blistered skin that she rates 9/10. How does the nurse interpret this?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The painless area is the least serious and needs no urgent care", "The painless area is full-thickness and the pain comes from surrounding partial-thickness burn", "The client is exaggerating the pain because the burn is insensate", "The painless area indicates a superficial burn that is already healing"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Full-thickness burns are painless because the nerve endings are destroyed</b>, while the partial-thickness skin around them hurts intensely. No pain does not mean not serious - that inversion is exactly what the item is testing."}, {"q": "An adult has deep partial-thickness burns to the entire anterior trunk and the whole of both arms. Using the rule of nines, what percentage of total body surface area is burned?", "type": "single", "opts": ["18%", "27%", "36%", "45%"], "ans": [2], "why": "Anterior trunk is <b>18%</b> and each whole arm is <b>9%</b>, so both arms add another 18%: <b>18 + 18 = 36%</b>. The adult rule of nines is worth committing to memory whole - head 9, each arm 9, each leg 18, anterior trunk 18, posterior trunk 18, perineum 1. Note that a burn of 36% is well past the 20% threshold at which formal fluid resuscitation and burn-center transfer are indicated.", "topic": "Burns", "icon": "🔥", "topicFull": "Burns", "card": "skin/NG-123_major-burns.html", "cardLabel": "Major Burns — Treatment"}, {"q": "Which findings during the emergent phase of a major burn need immediate intervention? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Singed nasal hair with a hoarse voice", "Urine output of 15 mL/hr", "Serum potassium 6.2 mEq/L", "Blood pressure 108/68 mm Hg", "Circumferential full-thickness burn of the chest"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "Singed nasal hair and hoarseness mean an <b>inhalation injury</b> and a threatened airway - intubate before edema closes it. Urine under 30 mL/hr says the fluid resuscitation is failing. Potassium rises early as damaged cells release it. A <b>circumferential chest burn</b> stops the chest wall expanding and needs escharotomy. The blood pressure is acceptable.", "topic": "Burns", "icon": "🔥", "topicFull": "Burns", "card": "skin/NG-123_major-burns.html", "cardLabel": "Major Burns — Treatment"}, {"q": "A client is 36 hours post major burn and urine output suddenly rises sharply. What does the nurse recognize?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The onset of acute kidney injury", "The expected fluid remobilization of the acute phase", "A sign of inadequate fluid resuscitation", "The development of sepsis"], "ans": [1], "why": "After roughly 24 to 48 hours the capillary leak seals and fluid moves <b>back into the vasculature</b>, so diuresis begins. That is the shift from the emergent to the acute phase. The risks now flip to <b>fluid overload and hypokalemia</b>, the opposite of the first day.", "topic": "Burns", "icon": "🔥", "topicFull": "Burns", "card": "skin/NG-123_major-burns.html", "cardLabel": "Major Burns — Treatment"}, {"q": "A client is burned at home at 0800 and arrives in the emergency department at 1000, when lactated Ringer's is started. Using the Parkland formula, by what time should the first half of the 24-hour volume be infused?", "type": "single", "opts": ["By 1600", "By 1800", "By midnight", "By 0800 the next morning"], "ans": [0], "why": "The first half of the Parkland volume must be in within <b>8 hours of the injury</b>, not 8 hours after arrival, so the deadline is 1600. Choosing 1800 times the clock from the ED arrival and would leave the client under-resuscitated for two extra hours."}, {"q": "A client is admitted with multiple scattered, irregular grease-splash burns across the anterior trunk, both thighs and one forearm. The pattern does not fit the rule of nines. Which method should the nurse use to estimate the burned surface area?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Use the client's palm including the fingers as approximately 1% TBSA", "Use the nurse's palm without the fingers as approximately 1% TBSA", "Assign the full 18% for the anterior trunk and 18% for both thighs together", "Round the scattered areas up to the nearest 9% using the rule of nines"], "ans": [0], "why": "For scattered irregular burns the estimate is made with <b>the client's own palm including the fingers, about 1% TBSA</b>. The nurse's palm is the tempting wrong answer because hand size varies between people, which is exactly why the client's hand is the standard."}, {"q": "A client admitted with 45 percent TBSA partial- and full-thickness burns is being prepared for fluid resuscitation. A new graduate asks which solution to hang for the calculated volume. Which IV fluid should the nurse obtain?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Lactated Ringer's", "0.9% sodium chloride", "5% dextrose in water", "5% dextrose in 0.45% sodium chloride"], "ans": [0], "why": "Parkland resuscitation is calculated and given as <b>lactated Ringer's</b> over the first 24 hours. Normal saline is the tempting answer because it is the default resuscitation fluid on most units, but burn resuscitation as taught here is LR, not NS and not a dextrose solution."}, {"q": "A client is admitted after a burn involving both legs, the buttocks and the perineum. There are no burns to the face, neck or chest, the voice is clear and no soot or singed hair is present. Which nursing action takes priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Establish IV access and begin the calculated lactated Ringer's infusion", "Prepare for early intubation before airway swelling develops", "Apply silver sulfadiazine to all of the burned areas", "Premedicate the client for the first dressing change"], "ans": [0], "why": "Burns from the <b>waist down make fluid the priority</b>; airway takes precedence only when the face, neck or chest is involved. Early intubation is the correct answer for an inhalation picture, but this client has none of the airway red flags, so delaying fluids to manage an airway that is not threatened is the error."}, {"q": "A client with a major burn is now past the emergent phase and is receiving daily wound care. Which priorities does the nurse address during the acute phase of burn care? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Monitoring the wounds for infection", "Providing aggressive nutrition support", "Managing pain, including premedication before every dressing change", "Calculating and titrating the Parkland fluid volume", "Beginning long-term management of scarring and contractures"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "The acute phase is <b>wound care, infection, nutrition and pain</b>. Parkland belongs to the emergent phase (0-48 hours) and scar and contracture work belongs to the rehabilitative phase, so both feel right to a student who has not separated the three phases."}, {"q": "A client is admitted with burns over approximately 65 percent TBSA. Which complications should the nurse monitor for over the course of this client's care? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Hypothermia", "Compartment syndrome", "Hypernatremia caused by the burn injury", "Fluid overload after the first 24 hours", "Hypokalemia caused by cell death"], "ans": [0, 1, 3], "why": "Very large burns bring <b>infection, hypothermia, compartment syndrome and fluid overload once the first 24 hours have passed</b>. The two electrolyte options are reversed: cell death drives sodium <b>down</b> and potassium <b>up</b>, so hypernatremia and hypokalemia are the traps."}], "5": [{"q": "A client in adrenal crisis has BP 78/44, confusion, potassium 6.1, severe abdominal pain, vomiting and diarrhea. Which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Give oral hydrocortisone with a sip of water", "Infuse a 0.9 percent sodium chloride bolus", "Administer oral potassium binder", "Obtain a dexamethasone suppression test"], "ans": [1], "why": "Crisis management starts with <b>fluids (and dextrose) before the definitive steroid</b>, and the instructors specifically wanted a 0.9 percent NS bolus. Every oral option is wrong here because she is vomiting and has diarrhea."}, {"q": "Four hours after a thyroidectomy, which finding does the nurse address first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A hoarse, weakening voice", "Positive Chvostek sign", "Sore throat when swallowing", "Serosanguineous drainage on the dressing"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Voice changes signal recurrent laryngeal nerve involvement and airway risk</b>, so they rank first; hypocalcemia (Chvostek, Trousseau, carpopedal spasm) comes next, then neck swelling. Chvostek looks more dramatic on paper but the airway outranks it."}, {"q": "A client has 6 L of pale urine in 24 hours, urine specific gravity 1.002, serum sodium 152 and BP 88/54. Which condition does the nurse suspect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Diabetes insipidus", "SIADH", "Cushing's syndrome", "Thyroid storm"], "ans": [0], "why": "Dilute high-volume urine with <b>low specific gravity and high serum sodium</b> is DI - dry inside, too little ADH. SIADH is the mirror: scant concentrated urine, high specific gravity, overload and low sodium."}, {"q": "A client with SIADH has a serum sodium of 120. Which interventions does the nurse include? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Fluid restriction", "Encourage 3 L of oral fluids daily", "Seizure precautions", "Neurologic checks", "Daily potassium supplementation"], "ans": [0, 2, 3], "why": "SIADH is treated with <b>fluid restriction plus diuretics and sodium</b>, and a sodium of 120 makes this a neuro problem - <b>seizure precautions and neuro checks</b> are required. Pushing fluids dilutes the sodium further; potassium is not the disordered electrolyte here."}, {"q": "Which laboratory pattern fits Addison's disease?", "type": "single", "opts": ["High sodium, high potassium, high glucose", "Low sodium, high potassium, low glucose", "Low sodium, low potassium, high glucose", "High sodium, low potassium, low glucose"], "ans": [1], "why": "Addison's is low cortisol and aldosterone: <b>low BP, low glucose, low sodium - but high potassium</b>. Cushing's is the mirror image on nearly every parameter except potassium, which inverts, and that single flip is usually the whole question."}, {"q": "A client taking levothyroxine has temp 104.9 F, HR 125, RR 42, restlessness and heat intolerance. Which intervention does the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Administer methimazole to block hormone synthesis", "Give a beta blocker, begin cooling, hold the next dose and notify the provider", "Increase the levothyroxine dose to stabilize the thyroid axis", "Start IV hydrocortisone and warming measures"], "ans": [1], "why": "This is iatrogenic hyperthyroidism from excess levothyroxine, so treatment is <b>beta blocker, cooling, low-stimulation room, hold the dose and notify</b>. Antithyroid drugs are useless because the excess T4 is already circulating, not being manufactured."}, {"q": "A client with Addison's disease is admitted with vomiting and hypotension. Which findings does the nurse expect? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Hyperkalemia", "Hyponatremia", "Hypoglycemia", "Hypertension", "Hyperpigmentation of skin creases"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "Addison's is <b>too little</b> of everything adrenal: aldosterone falls so sodium is lost and potassium is retained; cortisol falls so glucose falls; ACTH rises trying to flog the gland and <b>pigments the skin</b>. Hypertension belongs to Cushing's, the mirror image.", "topic": "Endocrine Disorders", "icon": "⚗️", "topicFull": "Endocrine Disorders", "card": "endo/NG-287_addisons-vs-cushings.html", "cardLabel": "Addison’s vs Cushing’s"}, {"q": "Which client statement indicates correct understanding of lifelong corticosteroid replacement?", "type": "single", "opts": ["\"I will stop the steroid if I get a stomach upset.\"", "\"If I am ill or having surgery, my dose will need to go up, not down.\"", "\"I can stop once my energy returns.\"", "\"I should take it at bedtime to avoid daytime side effects.\""], "ans": [1], "why": "The single most important teaching in adrenal insufficiency is <b>stress dosing</b>: illness, injury and surgery all require more, never less. Abruptly stopping precipitates <b>addisonian crisis</b>, which kills. Replacement is taken in the <b>morning</b> to mimic the natural cortisol peak.", "pharm": 1, "topic": "Endocrine Disorders", "icon": "⚗️", "topicFull": "Endocrine Disorders"}, {"q": "A client has SIADH. Which findings does the nurse expect? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Serum sodium 122 mEq/L", "Concentrated urine with high specific gravity", "Urine output of 6 L per day", "Weight gain without edema", "Serum osmolality below normal"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 4], "why": "SIADH is <b>too much</b> ADH: water is held, so the client dilutes - low sodium, low serum osmolality, concentrated urine, and weight gain from water that stays intracellular rather than forming pitting edema. Six liters of urine a day is <b>diabetes insipidus</b>, the opposite condition.", "topic": "Endocrine Disorders", "icon": "⚗️", "topicFull": "Endocrine Disorders", "card": "endo/NG-282_siadh-vs-di-1.html", "cardLabel": "SIADH vs DI (1)"}, {"q": "A client with hypothyroidism is prescribed levothyroxine. Which instructions are correct? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Take it in the morning on an empty stomach", "Separate it from calcium and iron by at least 4 hours", "Report palpitations, weight loss or heat intolerance", "Expect full symptom relief within 48 hours", "Do not switch brands without telling the provider"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "Levothyroxine needs an <b>empty stomach</b> and is blocked by calcium and iron. Palpitations and heat intolerance mean the dose has gone too high - the client has been made hyperthyroid. Brands differ in bioavailability. Relief takes <b>weeks</b>, not days, and expecting otherwise is why clients stop taking it.", "pharm": 1, "topic": "Endocrine Disorders", "icon": "⚗️", "topicFull": "Endocrine Disorders", "card": "endo/NG-285_hypothyroidism.html", "cardLabel": "Hypothyroidism"}, {"q": "A client with Hashimoto's hypothyroidism is admitted lethargic and difficult to arouse, hypothermic and bradycardic, with poor respiratory effort. Which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Support the client's airway and breathing", "Administer IV levothyroxine", "Begin an IV infusion of fluids with dextrose", "Apply warming blankets"], "ans": [0], "why": "In myxedema coma everything slows, and the module is explicit that <b>airway is the priority</b> when a hypothyroid client is lethargic with poor respiratory effort. Fluids with dextrose do come before IV levothyroxine, and warming is part of the treatment, but no drug helps a client who is not ventilating."}, {"q": "A client with pheochromocytoma has severe hypertension, headache and diaphoresis and is scheduled for adrenal surgery. The first dose of doxazosin is due. Which action is the priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Stay with the client and monitor for severe hypotension after the first dose", "Withhold the alpha blocker until after the adrenal tumor is removed", "Teach the client that the medication shrinks the tumor before surgery", "Expect the medication to raise the blood pressure toward normal"], "ans": [0], "why": "Alpha blockers — the <b>\"-osin\"</b> drugs such as doxazosin and terazosin — are started before surgery for this catecholamine-secreting tumor, and <b>severe first-dose hypotension</b> is the known risk to watch. Withholding until after surgery is the tempting wrong choice: the blockade must be established beforehand, not afterward."}, {"q": "A client reports months of fatigue, cold intolerance and constipation. Laboratory results show a low T4 with a low TSH. How does the nurse interpret this pattern?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The pituitary has failed, which is secondary hypothyroidism", "The thyroid gland itself has failed, which is primary hypothyroidism", "Hashimoto's thyroiditis is the most likely cause", "The client has been overtreated with thyroid replacement"], "ans": [0], "why": "Compare the gland hormone with its stimulating hormone: when both move the <b>same</b> direction, the problem is upstream, so low T4 with low TSH is pituitary, or secondary, failure. Primary hypothyroidism — including Hashimoto's — shows low T4 with a <b>high</b> TSH, because the pituitary is still pushing a thyroid that cannot answer."}, {"q": "A client who has taken high-dose oral steroids for months stopped them two days ago because he felt better. He is now weak, dizzy and hypotensive. Which explanation does the nurse give?", "type": "single", "opts": ["His adrenal glands have atrophied and cannot make cortisol, so the steroid must be tapered rather than stopped", "The dose was too high, and the symptoms will pass as the drug clears from his system", "He has developed Cushing's syndrome from the months of steroid therapy", "The symptoms are unrelated to the steroid and suggest an infection"], "ans": [0], "why": "Exogenous steroids suppress the body's own cortisol and the adrenals atrophy, which is why steroids are <b>always tapered, never stopped abruptly</b>; stopping leaves an acute cortisol deficit with weakness and hypotension. Waiting for the drug to clear is wrong — the problem is too <b>little</b> cortisol now, not too much."}, {"q": "A client admitted with Cushing's syndrome has moon face, a buffalo hump and central obesity with thin limbs. Which additional findings does the nurse expect? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Hypertension", "Hypokalemia", "Bronze hyperpigmentation of the skin", "Elevated blood glucose", "Hyponatremia"], "ans": [0, 1, 3], "why": "Cushing's is Addison's mirror image: too much cortisol raises blood pressure, glucose and sodium, and <b>potassium is the one value that inverts</b> and falls. Bronze skin and hyponatremia are the tempting picks, but both belong to Addison's, where cortisol is too low."}, {"q": "A client with Graves' disease is admitted with temp 103.8 F, HR 142, BP 168/88 and delirium. Which interventions does the nurse anticipate? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["IV fluids with dextrose", "A beta blocker for the cardiac symptoms", "External cooling measures", "IV levothyroxine", "Holding antithyroid medication until the fever resolves"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "This is thyroid storm: <b>fluids with dextrose come first</b>, then a beta blocker for the cardiac symptoms plus PTU or methimazole, plus cooling. IV levothyroxine is the trap — it treats the opposite crisis, myxedema coma — and the antithyroid drug is given, not held."}], "6": [{"q": "A client arrives with glucose 640, dry mucous membranes and confusion. Which assessment does the nurse perform first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Level of consciousness", "Hydration and circulatory status", "Repeat fingerstick glucose", "Urine ketones"], "ans": [1], "why": "In severe hyperglycemia the first assessment is <b>dehydration and circulation</b> - it sits in the ABCs, ahead of a fingerstick and ahead of LOC, because glucose is not A-B-C-D. Both crises start with IV fluids, then a regular insulin drip."}, {"q": "Which client does the nurse assess first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A client with glucose 386 and 2+ urine ketones ordered for an insulin drip", "A client with glucose 58 who is shaky, sweaty and confused", "A client with an A1C of 9.1 percent asking about diet", "A client with glucose 240 who missed a morning snack"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Hypoglycemia outranks a very high glucose in acuity</b> because she can lose consciousness within minutes. Awake at 58, she needs 15 g of fast carbohydrate (4 to 6 oz of juice) and a recheck in 15 minutes."}, {"q": "An insulin drip is ordered at 0.1 units/kg/hr for a client weighing 165 lb. The bag is 100 units in 100 mL. What rate does the nurse set?", "type": "single", "opts": ["4 mL/hr", "8 mL/hr", "15 mL/hr", "17 mL/hr"], "ans": [1], "why": "165 divided by 2.2 = 75 kg; 75 x 0.1 = 7.5 units/hr, and at 1 unit/mL that is 7.5, rounded to <b>8 mL/hr</b>. Answering 17 means the pounds were never converted - 2.2 always goes with the pounds."}, {"q": "A client is on a DKA insulin drip. Which actions does the nurse include? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Check glucose hourly", "Switch to a dextrose-containing fluid when glucose reaches 250", "Give sodium bicarbonate once glucose falls below 300", "Replace potassium as it normalizes and begins to fall", "Change to subcutaneous insulin as soon as ketones clear"], "ans": [0, 1, 3], "why": "The drip set is <b>hourly glucose, D5-containing fluid at 250 to avoid cerebral edema, and potassium replacement</b> because insulin drives K into cells. Bicarbonate is only for pH below 7.0, and subcutaneous insulin is never used during a crisis."}, {"q": "A client takes NPH insulin at 0700. When should the nurse teach her that a snack is most important?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Around 0715, when the insulin peaks", "Around 0900, 2 hours after the dose", "Around 1500, about 8 hours after the dose", "At bedtime only"], "ans": [2], "why": "NPH is the only intermediate insulin: onset 1 to 2 hours, <b>peak 6 to 8 hours</b>, so a snack belongs around the 8-hour mark. The 15-minute answer belongs to lispro and aspart, which are given immediately before a meal, never 30 minutes ahead."}, {"q": "A client with type 1 diabetes has influenza and is eating almost nothing. Which instruction is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Hold the insulin until she is eating normally again", "Take half the usual insulin dose while appetite is poor", "Keep taking insulin, check glucose every 2 to 4 hours and check ketones", "Stop insulin but check glucose every 2 hours"], "ans": [2], "why": "The sick-day rule is <b>keep taking insulin even when not eating</b>, monitor glucose every 2 to 4 hours, check ketones, hydrate, and call for glucose over 250 that is not falling or illness beyond 2 days. Illness raises glucose through stress hormones, so skipping or halving the dose drives her toward DKA."}, {"type": "sata", "week": "6", "step": "recognize", "topic": "Diabetes", "q": "A nurse is caring for a client who has type 1 diabetes and is admitted with diabetic ketoacidosis. Which findings should the nurse expect? (Select all that apply.)", "opts": ["Fruity breath odor", "Kussmaul respirations", "Blood glucose less than 70 mg/dL", "Abdominal pain and vomiting", "Serum bicarbonate above 24 mEq/L", "Polyuria and polydipsia"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 5], "why": "DKA classically produces a fruity breath odor from ketone buildup, Kussmaul respirations that blow off excess CO2 and acid, GI symptoms from ketosis and acidosis, and polyuria/polydipsia from osmotic diuresis. DKA causes hyperglycemia, not hypoglycemia, so a glucose under 70 mg/dL would not be expected. DKA causes metabolic acidosis with a low, not elevated, bicarbonate level, so a bicarbonate above 24 mEq/L is inconsistent with the diagnosis.", "src": "ATI Adult Med-Surg ch 83 (Complications of Diabetes Mellitus - DKA and HHS)", "icon": "🍬", "topicFull": "Diabetes", "card": "endo/NG-291_dka-vs-hhs.html", "cardLabel": "DKA vs HHS"}, {"q": "A client with type 1 diabetes has a blood glucose of 480 mg/dL, ketones in the urine, and deep rapid respirations. Which is the priority action?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Give the prescribed IV regular insulin bolus", "Begin IV 0.9% sodium chloride", "Administer sodium bicarbonate", "Give the client orange juice"], "ans": [1], "why": "In diabetic ketoacidosis the client is profoundly <b>volume depleted</b> from osmotic diuresis, and fluid comes first - it lowers glucose on its own and protects perfusion. Insulin follows, and only once <b>potassium is known</b>, because insulin drives potassium into cells and can stop the heart. Bicarbonate is reserved for severe acidosis.", "topic": "Diabetes", "icon": "🍬", "topicFull": "Diabetes", "card": "endo/NG-290_diabetes.html", "cardLabel": "Diabetes"}, {"q": "Which findings distinguish hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state from diabetic ketoacidosis? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Glucose often above 600 mg/dL", "Absent or minimal ketones", "Kussmaul respirations", "Usually occurs in type 2 diabetes", "Profound neurological changes"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 4], "why": "HHS is the <b>type 2</b> emergency: enough insulin to block ketone production but not enough to control glucose, so sugars go higher, ketones stay absent, and the picture is dominated by <b>dehydration and altered mental status</b>. <b>Kussmaul respirations belong to DKA</b> - they are the lungs blowing off acid, and without ketoacidosis there is no acid to blow off.", "topic": "Diabetes", "icon": "🍬", "topicFull": "Diabetes", "card": "endo/NG-291_dka-vs-hhs.html", "cardLabel": "DKA vs HHS"}, {"q": "A client takes NPH insulin at 0730. When is hypoglycemia most likely?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Around 0800", "Between 1200 and 1900", "Around 2300", "Immediately after the injection"], "ans": [1], "why": "NPH is <b>intermediate-acting</b>: onset 1 to 2 hours, <b>peak 4 to 12 hours</b>, duration 12 to 18. A 0730 dose peaks across the afternoon and early evening, so that is when the client is at risk - typically before dinner. Knowing peaks is how these questions are answered, not memorizing times.", "pharm": 1, "topic": "Diabetes", "icon": "🍬", "topicFull": "Diabetes", "card": "endo/NG-290_diabetes.html", "cardLabel": "Diabetes"}, {"q": "Which instructions are correct for a client with diabetes about sick days? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Continue taking insulin even if unable to eat", "Check blood glucose every 3 to 4 hours", "Check urine or blood ketones when glucose is above 240 mg/dL", "Hold all insulin until eating resumes", "Drink sugar-free fluids if glucose is high and sugar-containing fluids if it is low"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "Illness raises counter-regulatory hormones, so <b>glucose rises even when nothing is eaten</b>. Holding insulin during illness is how people arrive in DKA. Monitoring goes up, ketones get checked above 240, and fluid choice follows the glucose.", "topic": "Diabetes", "icon": "🍬", "topicFull": "Diabetes", "card": "endo/NG-290_diabetes.html", "cardLabel": "Diabetes"}, {"q": "A client with type 1 diabetes becomes shaky, diaphoretic and confused about an hour after her lispro dose. She is alert and able to swallow, and a fingerstick reads 58 mg/dL. Which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Give 4 oz of orange juice", "Administer 50 mL of D50 IV", "Recheck the glucose in 15 minutes before treating", "Hold the next insulin dose and notify the provider"], "ans": [0], "why": "For a hypoglycemic client who is awake and can swallow, the treatment is <b>15 g of fast carbohydrate</b> such as 4 oz of juice, followed by a recheck in 15 minutes. IV dextrose is reserved for the client who is unconscious or cannot swallow, and waiting 15 minutes before treating spends the exact time hypoglycemia does not give you."}, {"q": "A nurse finds a client with type 1 diabetes unresponsive and diaphoretic. The glucose is 42 mg/dL and the client has no IV access. Which action does the nurse take?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Administer IM glucagon", "Hold a cup of juice to the client's lips", "Place glucose tablets between the cheek and gum", "Recheck the glucose in 15 minutes"], "ans": [0], "why": "When the client is unconscious or cannot swallow, <b>nothing goes in the mouth</b> — the treatment is glucagon or IV dextrose, and with no IV access glucagon is the available route. Juice or tablets placed in the mouth of an unresponsive client risk aspiration, no matter how automatic the Rule of 15 feels."}, {"q": "A client with type 1 diabetes calls before a planned run and reports a glucose of 268 mg/dL with ketones in the urine. Which instruction does the nurse give?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Hold the run until the glucose is lower and the ketones have cleared", "Skip the insulin dose before the run to prevent hypoglycemia", "Run as planned, but carry a fast-acting carbohydrate", "Run as planned and check the glucose afterward"], "ans": [0], "why": "Exercise is <b>held when the glucose is above 250 mg/dL with ketones</b>, because exercising in that state pushes the client further toward ketoacidosis. Carrying fast-acting carbohydrate is correct advice for routine exercise but does not make this run safe, and insulin is never skipped before activity."}, {"q": "A nursing student prepares to mix NPH and regular insulin in one syringe. Which action by the student requires the nurse to intervene?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The student draws the cloudy NPH into the syringe before the clear regular insulin", "The student rolls the NPH vial between the palms instead of shaking it", "The student selects an injection site other than the one used for the last dose", "The student confirms that the regular insulin is clear before drawing it up"], "ans": [0], "why": "The rule for mixing is <b>clear before cloudy</b>, so regular insulin is drawn first and the cloudy NPH cannot contaminate the regular vial. Rolling rather than shaking the NPH is correct technique, since shaking foams the protein, so that action needs no correction."}, {"q": "A nurse is teaching a newly diagnosed client with type 1 diabetes who will take lispro with meals and glargine at bedtime. Which instructions does the nurse include? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Inject the lispro immediately before eating rather than 30 minutes ahead", "Rotate injection sites so lipodystrophy does not ruin absorption", "Never mix the glargine with another insulin in the same syringe", "Expect the glargine to peak about 6 hours after the bedtime dose", "Hold the insulin on any day illness keeps you from eating"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "Lispro starts working in <b>15 minutes</b>, so it is given immediately before the meal, sites are rotated to protect absorption, and glargine is <b>never mixed</b> with another insulin. Glargine has no peak at all, and the sick-day rule is to keep taking insulin even when not eating — holding it is the trap this course guarantees will be tested."}, {"q": "A client who takes glipizide becomes shaky and diaphoretic and reports a headache during morning care. She is awake and able to swallow. Which actions does the nurse take? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Check the blood glucose", "Give 15 g of fast carbohydrate such as 3 glucose tablets", "Recheck the glucose 15 minutes after treating", "Administer IM glucagon because the client is symptomatic", "Withhold anything by mouth until the provider returns the call"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "Shakiness, sweating and headache in a client on a <b>sulfonylurea</b> point straight to hypoglycemia: check the glucose, treat with 15 g of fast carbohydrate, and recheck in 15 minutes. Glucagon is for the client who is unconscious or cannot swallow, so it is the wrong route here, and withholding oral treatment lets a low glucose keep falling."}], "7": [{"q": "A client with a head injury has BP 82/50, HR 130, and a newly dilated sluggish left pupil. Which does the nurse address first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The dilated sluggish pupil, because it suggests herniation", "The hypotension and tachycardia", "Obtain a repeat GCS before intervening", "Elevate the head of the bed and recheck in 15 minutes"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>ABC before D, every time</b> - hypotension with tachycardia outranks the new pupil change, because perfusion failure kills the whole brain, not just one hemisphere. The pupil is the more alarming-looking finding, which is exactly why it is the distractor."}, {"q": "A client with elevated ICP begins decorticate posturing. Mannitol is ordered. What does the nurse do first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Elevate the head of the bed to 30 degrees", "Retrieve and prepare the mannitol", "Place the client in Trendelenburg", "Suction the airway to stimulate a response"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Raising the HOB to 30 degrees takes seconds and improves venous drainage</b>, while mannitol has to be fetched and prepared. Trendelenburg is never used with raised ICP, and suctioning transiently raises it further."}, {"q": "A client has an ICP of 22 mmHg and a MAP of 70 mmHg. What is the cerebral perfusion pressure, and how does the nurse interpret it?", "type": "single", "opts": ["92 mmHg, adequate", "48 mmHg, below goal", "48 mmHg, adequate", "22 mmHg, below goal"], "ans": [1], "why": "CPP = MAP - ICP = 70 - 22 = <b>48 mmHg, which is below the goal of greater than 50</b>. Normal ICP is 10 to 15, so this client's rising pressure is already starving the brain of perfusion."}, {"q": "Which findings would exclude a client from receiving t-PA for an ischemic stroke? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Current anticoagulant therapy", "Blood pressure 196/104", "Age 78", "Abdominal surgery 10 days ago", "Symptom onset 2 hours ago"], "ans": [0, 1, 3], "why": "Contraindications include <b>anticoagulant use, SBP over 180, recent bleed or surgery</b>, age under 18 and abnormal glucose. Age 78 is not a barrier, and 2 hours sits inside the 3 to 4.5 hour window - both are there to be mistaken for exclusions."}, {"q": "Which assessment finding is the earliest indicator of rising intracranial pressure?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A subtle change in level of consciousness", "Widening pulse pressure with bradycardia", "Cheyne-Stokes respirations", "Fixed dilated pupils"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>A change in LOC is the earliest sign</b>; Cushing's triad - rising systolic with widened pulse pressure, bradycardia and irregular respirations - is a <b>late herniation sign</b>. If the triad is present, you are already late."}, {"q": "A client with suspected stroke has a patent airway and adequate breathing. What does the nurse do next?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Administer chewable aspirin", "Perform a rapid focused neurologic assessment", "Start a heparin drip", "Give a bolus of isotonic fluid"], "ans": [1], "why": "After A and B are confirmed the next step is a <b>rapid neuro assessment of about 20 seconds</b> to establish deficits and time of onset. Aspirin cannot be given before the non-contrast CT distinguishes ischemic from hemorrhagic stroke."}, {"type": "sata", "week": "7", "step": "solutions", "topic": "Neurologic Dysfunction…", "q": "A nurse is planning care for a client newly diagnosed with generalized tonic-clonic epilepsy. Which interventions should the nurse include in the plan of care? (Select all that apply.)", "opts": ["Keep oxygen and suction equipment set up at the bedside.", "Pad the side rails and keep the bed in its lowest position.", "Store a padded tongue blade at the bedside to insert if a seizure starts.", "Teach the client to take the antiseizure medication at the same clock time each day.", "Teach the client to avoid swimming alone, ladders, and contact sports.", "Plan to hold the client's arms and legs still if jerking movements begin."], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 4], "why": "A seizure plan protects the airway and prevents injury, so oxygen, suction, padded rails, and a low bed belong in it. Steady daily dosing keeps drug levels therapeutic, and activity limits protect the client if a seizure hits without warning. Nothing is ever placed in the mouth during a seizure, because it can break teeth or block the airway. Restraining a moving limb does not stop the seizure and can tear muscle or fracture bone.", "src": "ATI ch 7 Seizures and Epilepsy", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "A client arrives with left-sided weakness and slurred speech that began 90 minutes ago. Which action is the priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Obtain a non-contrast CT of the head", "Give aspirin 325 mg", "Start a heparin infusion", "Insert a nasogastric tube for medications"], "ans": [0], "why": "Nothing happens until a <b>non-contrast CT</b> separates ischemic from hemorrhagic stroke, because the treatments are opposites: give a thrombolytic in a bleed and you kill the client. The alteplase window is <b>4.5 hours</b>, so at 90 minutes the clock is the priority. Aspirin and heparin both wait for the scan, and nothing goes by mouth until swallowing is screened.", "topic": "Neurologic Dysfunction…", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "Which findings suggest a stroke affecting the right hemisphere? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Left-sided hemiparesis", "Left visual field neglect", "Impulsive behavior and poor safety judgment", "Expressive aphasia", "Overestimation of one's own abilities"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "The brain crosses, so a right-hemisphere stroke weakens the <b>left</b> side and neglects the <b>left</b> field. The behavioral signature is the dangerous part: <b>impulsive, unaware of the deficit, overestimating ability</b>, which is why these clients fall. <b>Aphasia is a left-hemisphere</b> problem for almost everyone.", "topic": "Neurologic Dysfunction…", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "A client with increased intracranial pressure is being cared for. Which nursing actions are appropriate? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Keep the head of the bed at 30 degrees with the head midline", "Cluster nursing care into long uninterrupted sessions", "Keep the environment quiet and dim", "Avoid hip flexion and neck rotation", "Instruct the client to exhale while turning rather than hold the breath"], "ans": [0, 2, 3, 4], "why": "Everything is aimed at <b>letting blood drain out of the head</b>: 30 degrees, head midline, no neck rotation, no hip flexion, and no Valsalva. Care is <b>spaced out, not clustered</b> - stacked stimulation stacks the pressure, which is the one that catches people out.", "topic": "Neurologic Dysfunction…", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders", "card": "neuro/NG-308_increased-icp.html", "cardLabel": "Increased Intracranial Pressure"}, {"q": "Which finding in a client with a head injury most concerns the nurse?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Blood pressure 168/56 mm Hg with a heart rate of 48/min", "Headache rated 5 out of 10", "Blood pressure 128/76 mm Hg with a heart rate of 88/min", "Reports of feeling tired"], "ans": [0], "why": "A <b>widening pulse pressure with bradycardia</b> - and irregular respirations - is <b>Cushing's triad</b>, a late and ominous sign of brainstem compression from rising intracranial pressure. It means herniation is close, and it is reported immediately.", "topic": "Neurologic Dysfunction…", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "A client admitted 4 hours ago with a left-hemisphere ischemic stroke is alert and follows commands. She asks for water and her morning oral medications. Which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Offer small sips of water while the client sits fully upright", "Hold all oral intake until a formal swallow screen is completed", "Crush the medications into applesauce to make them easier to swallow", "Have the family feed the client slowly and watch for coughing"], "ans": [1], "why": "Nothing goes by mouth after a stroke — <b>not even water or medications</b> — until swallowing is formally assessed, because aspiration pneumonia is the leading cause of death after stroke and a left-brain stroke brings language and swallowing deficits. Crushing the pills in applesauce is tempting because it feels safer, but it is still oral intake before the swallow screen."}, {"q": "A client admitted for seizure monitoring has been in a generalized tonic-clonic seizure for 7 minutes without regaining consciousness. The airway is protected and she is positioned on her side. Which intervention does the nurse anticipate first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Administering IV lorazepam", "Administering IV fosphenytoin", "Administering IV levetiracetam", "Inserting a padded airway between the teeth"], "ans": [0], "why": "A seizure lasting longer than 5 minutes is status epilepticus, and <b>IV lorazepam is given first</b>; fosphenytoin or levetiracetam follows to keep the seizure from returning. Fosphenytoin is the tempting choice because it is the drug that maintains control, but it is not the first agent — and nothing is ever placed in the mouth during a seizure."}, {"q": "A client with a traumatic brain injury and an ICP of 24 mmHg receives IV mannitol. Two hours later, which finding best tells the nurse the drug is having its intended effect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A marked increase in urine output", "A heart rate that has fallen to 52 beats per minute", "A systolic blood pressure that has risen with a widened pulse pressure", "Respirations that have become irregular and Cheyne-Stokes"], "ans": [0], "why": "Mannitol pulls fluid off the brain and that fluid is excreted, so <b>increased urine output</b> is how the nurse confirms it is working. The other three findings together are Cushing's triad — a late herniation sign meaning the ICP is still climbing, so they show the opposite of the intended effect."}, {"q": "A client arrives with sudden onset of \"the worst headache of my life,\" vomiting, and photophobia. Non-contrast CT shows blood in the subarachnoid space. Which prescription does the nurse question?", "type": "single", "opts": ["IV t-PA", "A calcium channel blocker to prevent vasospasm", "An IV antihypertensive to lower the blood pressure", "Coagulation studies with reversal of anticoagulation"], "ans": [0], "why": "\"Worst headache of my life\" with blood on CT is a hemorrhagic bleed, and <b>t-PA is never given</b> — it would extend the hemorrhage. The antihypertensive is the tempting one to question because lowering pressure seems risky for perfusion, but lowering the BP, reversing coags, and calcium channel blockers for vasospasm are all correct here."}, {"q": "A client is 6 hours post carotid endarterectomy. Which findings does the nurse report to the surgeon immediately? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Fullness and swelling on the operative side of the neck", "New hoarseness when the client speaks", "Blood pressure of 186/98 mmHg", "Incisional soreness when the client turns her head", "A dry mouth after being NPO for surgery"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "The three things watched for after endarterectomy are <b>hypertension, neck swelling, and hoarseness</b> — swelling threatens the airway, hoarseness signals nerve injury, and a systolic over 180 threatens the fresh suture line. Incisional soreness and a dry mouth after NPO status are expected findings and do not need the surgeon."}, {"q": "A client admitted with an ischemic stroke is being monitored on the stroke unit. Which findings require the nurse to notify the provider? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Blood pressure of 192/104 mmHg", "Blood pressure of 156/114 mmHg", "SpO2 of 89% on room air", "Blood pressure of 162/98 mmHg", "A Glasgow Coma Scale score that has fallen from 14 to 10"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "Stroke care calls for notification when the <b>systolic exceeds 180 or the diastolic exceeds 110</b>, when SpO2 drops below 92%, and when the level of consciousness changes — a GCS drop from 14 to 10 is the reportable event. A BP of 162/98 looks alarming but sits under both thresholds, so it is monitored rather than reported."}], "8": [{"q": "A client with a T4 spinal cord injury has BP 208/112, HR 48, a pounding headache and flushing above the injury. What does the nurse do first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Administer the prescribed antihypertensive", "Sit the client upright", "Check for a fecal impaction", "Lay the client flat and elevate the legs"], "ans": [1], "why": "In autonomic dysreflexia the first action is to <b>sit her up</b>, which uses gravity to drop the blood pressure immediately, then find and remove the trigger - check the bladder before the bowel. Antihypertensives are an adjunct, not the first move, and lying flat worsens the hypertension."}, {"q": "On the first day after spinal surgery, which finding does the nurse report immediately?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Blood pressure drifting from 122/78 to 110/70", "Pain rated 4 then 5 then 4", "Progressive left leg weakness now unable to lift the leg", "Requesting a second pillow"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Progressive motor loss distal to the surgical site is the concerning trend</b> and suggests cord compromise. Small BP drift and pain oscillating between 4 and 5 are noise - trend items reward spotting the finding that moves in one direction."}, {"q": "A client with myasthenia gravis takes pyridostigmine. Which instruction is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Take it with food to reduce nausea", "Take it 30 to 60 minutes before meals", "Take it only on days when weakness is noticeable", "Take it at bedtime to prevent morning weakness"], "ans": [1], "why": "Pyridostigmine is timed <b>30 to 60 minutes before meals</b> so its 3 to 4 hour peak covers chewing and swallowing. It is never stopped because she feels better - feeling better is the drug working."}, {"q": "A client is admitted with suspected bacterial meningitis. Which nursing actions are appropriate? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Initiate droplet precautions", "Place the client in a negative-pressure room with N95 use", "Elevate the head of the bed 30 degrees", "Assess for a petechial rash on the trunk and extremities", "Keep the room brightly lit for frequent neuro checks"], "ans": [0, 2, 3], "why": "Bacterial (meningococcal) meningitis needs <b>droplet precautions, HOB at 30 degrees, and monitoring for a petechial rash with a falling LOC</b>. Negative pressure and N95 belong to airborne conditions, and photophobia makes a bright room actively unkind."}, {"q": "A client has sudden one-sided facial droop. Which finding points to Bell's palsy rather than a stroke?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The forehead is involved and she cannot raise that eyebrow", "The forehead is spared and the eyebrow raises normally", "She has slurred speech with the droop", "The droop is accompanied by arm weakness"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Bell's palsy takes the whole side of the face including the forehead; a stroke spares the forehead.</b> Protect the affected eye with lubricant and a night patch - about 95 percent recover over months."}, {"q": "A client is placed in a halo device. Which nursing action is the priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Perform neurovascular checks of all extremities", "Loosen the vest bars slightly for comfort", "Use the vest bars to reposition the client in bed", "Clean the pin sites with an alcohol-based solution"], "ans": [0], "why": "The priority is <b>neurovascular checks of all extremities</b> - the device exists to protect the cord, and circulation and function tell you it is doing so. Never lift or turn a client by the vest bars, never remove the device, and keep a wrench taped to the vest for emergency CPR access."}, {"q": "A client with a spinal cord injury at T4 suddenly develops a pounding headache, flushing above the injury, and a blood pressure of 202/110 mm Hg. Which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Sit the client upright", "Administer the prescribed antihypertensive", "Check for a kinked urinary catheter", "Notify the provider"], "ans": [0], "why": "This is <b>autonomic dysreflexia</b>, and it can stroke the client within minutes. <b>Sitting upright is first</b> because gravity alone drops the blood pressure immediately and costs nothing. Then find and remove the trigger - a blocked catheter is the commonest, followed by impaction and skin pressure. Drugs come after position and trigger removal.", "topic": "Traumatic, Infectious,…", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "Which findings would the nurse expect in a client with myasthenia gravis? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Ptosis and diplopia worse at the end of the day", "Muscle weakness that improves with rest", "Symptoms that improve after edrophonium", "Ascending weakness beginning in the legs", "Difficulty chewing toward the end of a meal"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "Myasthenia gravis is a <b>descending, fatigable</b> weakness: worst with use, best with rest, and it hits the eyes and the muscles of chewing and swallowing first. <b>Ascending weakness from the legs is Guillain-Barré</b>, which is the pairing these questions test.", "topic": "Traumatic, Infectious,…", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro", "card": "neuro/NG-251_myasthenia-gravis.html", "cardLabel": "Myasthenia Gravis"}, {"q": "A client with Parkinson's disease is prescribed carbidopa-levodopa. Which client statement needs correction?", "type": "single", "opts": ["\"My urine and sweat may darken.\"", "\"I will take it with a high-protein meal so it does not upset my stomach.\"", "\"I should stand up slowly.\"", "\"It may take several weeks before I notice a real difference.\""], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Dietary protein competes with levodopa for absorption</b>, so a high-protein meal blunts the dose - clients are taught to separate them. Darkened urine and sweat are harmless, orthostatic hypotension is real and common, and the full effect does take weeks.", "pharm": 1, "topic": "Traumatic, Infectious,…", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro", "card": "neuro/NG-258_parkinsons.html", "cardLabel": "Parkinson's Disease"}, {"q": "A client is admitted with suspected bacterial meningitis. Which actions are priorities? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Place the client on droplet precautions", "Give the prescribed antibiotic as soon as cultures are drawn", "Keep the room bright to help with orientation", "Assess neurological status frequently", "Position for comfort and reduce stimulation"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 4], "why": "Bacterial meningitis needs <b>droplet precautions</b> and antibiotics <b>within the hour</b> - delay costs lives, so cultures are drawn fast and the drug is not held for results. Photophobia is a hallmark, so the room is kept <b>dark and quiet</b>, not bright.", "topic": "Traumatic, Infectious,…", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro", "card": "neuro/NG-252_meningitis.html", "cardLabel": "Meningitis"}, {"q": "A client is brought in after a major fall. He was briefly unconscious at the scene, then woke and talked normally with the paramedics for about 30 minutes. He is now difficult to arouse. Which injury does the nurse suspect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Diffuse axonal injury", "Epidural hematoma", "Subdural hematoma", "Spinal shock"], "ans": [1], "why": "The <b>lucid interval</b> after major trauma — out, awake and fine, then out again — is the signature of an arterial epidural bleed, which moves fast. Subdural is the tempting answer, but it is venous and builds slowly over 24–48 hours to 2 weeks, typically in older adults on anticoagulants or with alcohol use."}, {"q": "A client with a suspected cervical spinal cord injury from a diving accident stops breathing while the nurse is at the bedside. Which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Apply a cervical collar before addressing the airway", "Log roll the client onto a backboard", "Open the airway with a jaw thrust", "Open the airway with a head-tilt chin-lift"], "ans": [2], "why": "With a suspected spinal cord injury the airway is opened with a <b>jaw thrust</b>, which does not move the neck. The head-tilt chin-lift is the familiar maneuver and is exactly what must be avoided here, because extending the cervical spine can worsen the cord injury; collar and backboard follow the ABCs, not precede them."}, {"q": "A client admitted with Guillain-Barre syndrome after a recent infection now has weakness that has ascended from the feet to the upper thighs. Which assessment is the priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Pulse oximetry readings", "Vital capacity", "Deep tendon reflexes in the legs", "Ability to ambulate safely"], "ans": [1], "why": "Guillain-Barre ascends, so the threat is that the paralysis reaches the diaphragm and ventilation fails; <b>vital capacity</b> is what detects that early. Pulse oximetry looks like the respiratory answer, but oxygen saturation falls late — after respiratory failure is already underway."}, {"q": "An 82-year-old client admitted two days ago for a urinary tract infection becomes confused overnight, with attention that drifts in and out. Her family says her thinking was completely normal last week. Which interpretation by the nurse is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["An expected age-related memory change", "Dementia, which is gradual and irreversible", "Delirium, likely from the infection, and reversible", "Early Alzheimer's disease, since memory fails first"], "ans": [2], "why": "Confusion that comes on <b>suddenly</b> over hours to days, fluctuates and impairs attention is delirium until proven otherwise — it is reversible and UTI in an older adult is a classic cause. Dementia is the tempting choice, but it develops gradually over months to years and takes memory first, not attention."}, {"q": "A client is brought to the emergency department after a motorcycle crash. Which findings would lead the nurse to suspect a basilar skull fracture? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Bradycardia with hypotension", "Bruising behind the ear", "A ring of clear fluid around blood on the head dressing", "Pain when the flexed knee is straightened", "Bruising around both eyes"], "ans": [1, 2, 4], "why": "Battle's sign (bruising behind the ear), raccoon eyes and the <b>halo sign</b> — CSF ringing the blood on the dressing — are the three findings of a basilar skull fracture. Bradycardia with hypotension is neurogenic shock from lost sympathetic tone, and pain on straightening the flexed knee is Kernig's sign of meningitis."}, {"q": "A client is admitted to the intensive care unit with an acute cervical spinal cord injury. Which nursing actions are appropriate? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Reposition the client in bed every 2 hours", "Use a head-tilt chin-lift if the airway must be opened", "Maintain the mean arterial pressure at 85 mmHg or higher", "Log roll the client, maintaining spinal alignment", "Reposition every 4 hours once the client is up in a wheelchair"], "ans": [0, 2, 3], "why": "Cord perfusion is protected by keeping <b>MAP at 85 mmHg or higher</b>, skin by turning every 2 hours in bed, and alignment by log rolling. Every 4 hours sounds reasonable but wheelchair repositioning is hourly — pressure injuries can form within 6 hours — and the airway is opened with a jaw thrust, never a head-tilt chin-lift."}], "9": [{"q": "Fifteen minutes into a transfusion a client has chills, fever and flank pain. What does the nurse do first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Notify the provider", "Stop the transfusion", "Slow the rate and reassess in 15 minutes", "Administer the prescribed antipyretic"], "ans": [1], "why": "For any suspected reaction the sequence is <b>stop the transfusion, keep the line open with 0.9 percent NS on new tubing, stay with the client, then notify</b>. Slowing the rate keeps incompatible blood going in, and notifying first delays the one action that stops the exposure."}, {"q": "A client with immune thrombocytopenia has four findings. Which one does the nurse act on first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Scattered petechiae over both shins", "A nosebleed that lasted 10 minutes and stopped", "Severe headache with vomiting", "Bruising on the forearms"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Severe headache with vomiting in a thrombocytopenic client means intracranial hemorrhage until proven otherwise.</b> Petechiae and a nosebleed that has already stopped are expected findings that can wait."}, {"q": "Which statement about administering packed red blood cells is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Prime with lactated Ringer's and complete within 6 hours", "Prime with 0.9 percent sodium chloride, stay with the client the first 15 minutes, and infuse within 4 hours", "Prime with D5W and check vital signs every hour", "Stay with the client the first 5 minutes and infuse within 2 hours"], "ans": [1], "why": "Only <b>0.9 percent NS</b> is compatible - dextrose hemolyzes cells and LR clots them - and the nurse stays for the <b>first 15 minutes</b> with the unit completed within <b>4 hours</b>. Two-nurse verification precedes all of it."}, {"q": "On day 7 of heparin therapy a client's platelets drop from 220,000 to 90,000 in 48 hours. Which actions does the nurse take? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Stop the heparin", "Administer vitamin K", "Obtain daily platelet counts", "Start warfarin immediately", "Assess for signs of thrombosis"], "ans": [0, 2, 4], "why": "This is HIT: <b>stop the heparin, follow daily platelet counts, notify, and assess for clot</b>, because HIT clots as well as bleeds. Vitamin K is warfarin's antidote, and starting warfarin now is contraindicated - the falling platelet count makes bleeding look like the only risk, which is the trap."}, {"q": "A client develops respiratory distress during a transfusion. Which finding distinguishes TACO from TRALI?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Fever and chills", "Crackles, jugular venous distention and hypertension", "Urticaria and itching", "Hemoglobinuria and flank pain"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>TACO shows fluid-overload signs - crackles, JVD, hypertension - while TRALI produces respiratory distress without them.</b> The discriminator is overload, not the presence of dyspnea, which both share."}, {"q": "A client with hemophilia has a swollen, painful knee after a fall. Which intervention does the nurse include?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Apply heat and begin gentle range of motion", "Elevate the joint, apply ice and give factor replacement", "Give aspirin for pain and encourage ambulation", "Wrap the joint and perform passive range of motion hourly"], "ans": [1], "why": "Hemarthrosis is managed with <b>elevation, ice, factor replacement and no range of motion</b>; aspirin and NSAIDs are never given. Heat and movement both increase bleeding into the joint - and joint bleeds are about 75 percent of all bleeds in hemophilia."}, {"q": "A client is receiving a unit of packed red blood cells. Fifteen minutes in, the client reports low back pain and chills, and the urine turns dark. What is the nurse's first action?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Slow the transfusion and continue observing", "Stop the transfusion and keep the line open with normal saline", "Give the prescribed antihistamine", "Obtain a urine specimen for analysis"], "ans": [1], "why": "Back pain, chills and dark urine early in a transfusion mean an <b>acute hemolytic reaction</b>. <b>Stop the blood, keep the vein open with saline through new tubing</b>, then notify and investigate. Slowing it is not enough - every further milliliter adds hemolysis. Antihistamines treat mild allergic reactions, which is a different picture.", "topic": "Hematologic Disorders", "icon": "🩸", "topicFull": "Hematologic Disorders"}, {"q": "Which laboratory findings would the nurse expect in iron-deficiency anemia? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Low ferritin", "Microcytic, hypochromic red cells", "Elevated total iron-binding capacity", "Macrocytic red cells", "Low mean corpuscular volume"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "Iron deficiency makes cells <b>small and pale</b> - low MCV, low ferritin - and the body raises its iron-binding capacity trying to catch more. <b>Macrocytic</b> cells point the other way, to <b>B12 or folate</b> deficiency, and separating those two anemias is worth a mark on every hematology exam.", "topic": "Hematologic Disorders", "icon": "🩸", "topicFull": "Hematologic Disorders"}, {"q": "A client has disseminated intravascular coagulation. Which findings does the nurse expect? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Prolonged PT and aPTT", "Low platelet count", "Elevated D-dimer", "Elevated fibrinogen", "Bleeding from IV sites with simultaneous clotting"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "DIC is the paradox: <b>clotting and bleeding at once</b>. Clotting consumes platelets and factors, so counts fall and times lengthen; the clot breakdown raises D-dimer. <b>Fibrinogen falls, not rises</b> - it is being used up - and that is the option most often chosen wrongly.", "topic": "Hematologic Disorders", "icon": "🩸", "topicFull": "Hematologic Disorders"}, {"q": "A client with sickle cell disease is admitted in vaso-occlusive crisis. Which interventions are appropriate? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["IV fluids", "Prescribed opioid analgesia on a schedule", "Supplemental oxygen if hypoxic", "Cold compresses to painful joints", "Assessment for infection"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "Crisis care is <b>hydration, oxygenation and real pain control</b> - these clients are chronically undertreated for pain, and scheduled dosing beats chasing it. Infection is a common trigger. <b>Cold causes vasoconstriction and worsens sickling</b>; warmth is applied instead.", "topic": "Hematologic Disorders", "icon": "🩸", "topicFull": "Hematologic Disorders", "card": "cardio/NG-344_sickle-cell-hemophilia.html", "cardLabel": "Sickle Cell & Hemophilia"}, {"q": "A client admitted with multiple myeloma has an elevated serum calcium level and reports worsening back pain. Which nursing action is the priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Restrict fluids to reduce the workload on the kidneys", "Apply heat to the painful areas of the spine", "Encourage a high fluid intake to protect the kidneys", "Have the client ambulate independently to strengthen the bones"], "ans": [2], "why": "Myeloma follows <b>CRAB</b> (calcium up, renal failure, anemia, bone lesions), and pushing fluids is what protects the kidneys from the elevated calcium. Restricting fluids is tempting once renal failure is mentioned, but it concentrates the calcium and worsens the kidney injury; bone lesions also call for fall and fracture precautions, not independent ambulation."}, {"q": "A client with hemophilia is bleeding briskly from a venipuncture site on the forearm. What does the nurse do first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Administer the prescribed clotting factor concentrate", "Hold firm compression over the site", "Apply a warm pack to the forearm", "Draw a CBC to check the platelet count"], "ans": [1], "why": "When the client is bleeding <b>right now</b>, compression beats a factor injection. Factor replacement is the definitive treatment and is given before and after procedures, but it does not stop active bleeding this minute; heat is wrong (use ice), and a CBC is the wrong lab because platelets are normal in hemophilia — coags are the priority labs."}, {"q": "A client admitted with pernicious anemia reports numbness and tingling in both feet and an unsteady gait. Which teaching point is most important for the nurse to reinforce?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Oral vitamin B12 taken with vitamin C will correct the deficiency", "A folate supplement will resolve the numbness and gait problems", "Vitamin B12 will be needed as lifelong intramuscular injections", "The symptoms will resolve once the red blood cell size normalizes"], "ans": [2], "why": "Pernicious anemia is a lack of <b>intrinsic factor</b>, so oral B12 cannot be absorbed and lifelong IM injections are required. Folate is the tempting choice because B12 and folate deficiency look identical on a blood count, but folate deficiency does not produce neurologic signs — the numbness and gait changes are what identify this as B12."}, {"q": "A client admitted with polycythemia vera has a ruddy face, splenomegaly, and reports intense itching after a warm shower. Which nursing action is the priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Institute bleeding precautions because of the high cell counts", "Prepare to transfuse a unit of packed red blood cells", "Administer subcutaneous epoetin alfa as prescribed", "Encourage fluids and ambulation to reduce the risk of clot formation"], "ans": [3], "why": "In polycythemia vera the marrow <b>overproduces</b> red cells, so the blood is thick and the nursing priority is clot prevention while therapeutic phlebotomy remains the mainstay of treatment. Transfusion and epoetin alfa treat anemia — the opposite problem — and this client's risk is clotting, not bleeding."}, {"q": "A client admitted with leukemia has a platelet count of 18,000/mm³. Which interventions does the nurse include in the plan of care? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Give ibuprofen as needed for bone pain", "Provide an electric razor for shaving", "Give scheduled medications by the intramuscular route", "Administer a stool softener to prevent straining", "Provide a soft toothbrush for oral care"], "ans": [1, 3, 4], "why": "Thrombocytopenia precautions are an electric razor, a soft toothbrush, stool softeners to prevent straining, and checking stool for occult blood; a count under 20,000 is a significant bleeding risk. <b>NSAIDs, aspirin, and heparin are avoided</b>, and injections and IV sticks are minimized rather than used routinely — ibuprofen looks reasonable for bone pain but it worsens bleeding."}, {"q": "A client admitted with iron-deficiency anemia is being discharged on oral ferrous sulfate. Which client statements indicate correct understanding of the teaching? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["I will take the iron with a glass of orange juice.", "I will stop the iron and call the clinic if my stools turn black.", "I will take it on an empty stomach unless it upsets my stomach.", "I will sip the liquid form slowly from a cup.", "I should expect my stools to turn black and tarry."], "ans": [0, 2, 4], "why": "Vitamin C doubles iron absorption, iron is best on an empty stomach (with food only if it upsets the gut), and <b>black tarry stools are expected</b>, not a sign of bleeding — so stopping the drug for that reason is wrong. Liquid iron stains the teeth and must be taken through a straw rather than sipped from a cup."}], "10": [{"q": "Which client on the oncology unit does the nurse see first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A client with ANC 800 and a temperature of 100.8 F", "A client vomiting 3 hours after cisplatin", "A client with new petechiae on both forearms", "A client requesting the next dose of pain medication"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Neutropenic fever is the one situation where antibiotics are given without a proven infection</b> - cultures plus IV antibiotics, likely admission. ANC under 1,500 is neutropenic and under 500 is severe; with no neutrophils, a low-grade fever may be the only sign of overwhelming sepsis."}, {"q": "A vesicant chemotherapy agent is infusing peripherally when the site becomes swollen and the client reports burning. What does the nurse do first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Flush the line with saline to check patency", "Stop the infusion", "Remove the catheter and apply pressure", "Apply a warm compress to disperse the drug"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Stop the infusion first</b>, then notify the provider, give the antidote subcutaneously around the site and attempt to aspirate - do not remove the catheter and <b>do not flush</b>. Ice is correct because it localizes the drug; heat spreads it."}, {"q": "A client has a brachytherapy implant in place. Which action protects staff correctly?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Remain at least 6 feet away except when providing care, limit time in the room, and rotate caregivers", "Assign the same nurse each shift to maintain continuity", "Wear a shared dosimeter kept at the nurses' station", "Send soiled linens to the laundry immediately after each care episode"], "ans": [0], "why": "Brachytherapy safety is <b>distance of at least 6 feet, a 30-minute maximum exposure, rotating caregivers</b>, a personal dosimeter, a lead apron, and no pregnant staff assigned. Linens and dressings stay in the room, and continuity of assignment - normally a virtue - is exactly backwards here."}, {"q": "Which teaching points does the nurse give a neutropenic client? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Sushi from a reputable restaurant is acceptable", "Discard drinks left sitting out longer than 1 hour", "Avoid gardening and changing the cat litter", "Report any temperature over 100 F", "Fresh raw fruit is encouraged for vitamin intake"], "ans": [1, 2, 3], "why": "Neutropenic teaching means <b>no raw food regardless of the restaurant, discard drinks left out over 1 hour, avoid gardening and cat litter, and report a temp over 100 F</b>. Quality of the kitchen does not change the microbial load on raw fish."}, {"q": "A client receiving curative chemotherapy asks about palliative care. Which response is accurate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Palliative care can begin now, alongside your treatment", "Palliative care starts only when treatment stops working", "Palliative care requires a prognosis of 6 months or less", "Palliative care and hospice are the same program"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Palliative care runs alongside curative treatment at any stage; hospice requires a terminal prognosis of about 6 months or less and a shift away from curative treatment.</b> Conflating the two is asked constantly."}, {"q": "Two days after starting chemotherapy for a bulky lymphoma, which lab pattern indicates tumor lysis syndrome?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Low potassium, low phosphorus, high calcium, low uric acid", "High potassium, high phosphorus, low calcium, high uric acid", "High potassium, low phosphorus, high calcium, normal uric acid", "Normal potassium, high phosphorus, high calcium, high uric acid"], "ans": [1], "why": "Lysed cells dump <b>potassium, phosphorus and uric acid up, with calcium down</b> as phosphate binds it, risking arrhythmias and AKI. Prevention and treatment are aggressive IV fluids plus allopurinol or rasburicase - methotrexate is chemotherapy, not a treatment."}, {"type": "sata", "week": "10", "step": "action", "topic": "Oncologic Disorders…", "q": "A nurse is caring for a client who has an absolute neutrophil count of 400/mm3 following chemotherapy. Which actions should the nurse take? (Select all that apply.)", "opts": ["Place the client in a private room", "Have the client wear a mask when leaving the room for transport", "Assign the client a roommate who is also immunosuppressed", "Perform hand hygiene before and after every client contact", "Take a rectal temperature to obtain the most accurate reading", "Report a temperature above 37.8° C (100° F) to the provider immediately"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 5], "why": "Neutropenic precautions include a private room, a mask for transport, strict hand hygiene, and avoiding unnecessary invasive procedures, with any fever above 37.8° C (100° F) reported to the provider immediately. Assigning a roommate who is also immunosuppressed increases cross-infection risk rather than reducing it, and a rectal temperature is an invasive procedure that risks mucosal trauma and introducing infection in a neutropenic client, so it should be avoided.", "src": "ATI Adult Med-Surg ch 91 (Cancer Treatment Options)", "icon": "🎗️", "topicFull": "Oncologic Disorders & End-of-Life Care", "card": "core/NG-314_neutropenic-precautions-palliative.html", "cardLabel": "Neutropenic Precautions & Palliative Care"}, {"q": "A client receiving chemotherapy has an absolute neutrophil count of 400/mm³. Which instruction is most important?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Avoid crowds and anyone who is unwell", "Use a soft toothbrush and an electric razor", "Increase dietary iron", "Rest frequently throughout the day"], "ans": [0], "why": "An ANC under 500 is <b>severe neutropenia</b>, and infection is what kills these clients - a fever becomes an emergency. Soft toothbrush and electric razor are the <b>thrombocytopenia</b> precautions, and rest addresses anemia. Matching the precaution to the cell line that is low is the skill being tested.", "topic": "Oncologic Disorders…", "icon": "🎗️", "topicFull": "Oncologic Disorders & End-of-Life Care", "card": "core/NG-314_neutropenic-precautions-palliative.html", "cardLabel": "Neutropenic Precautions & Palliative Care"}, {"q": "Which statements about a client's advance directive are correct? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["It takes effect only when the client cannot make decisions", "The client may revoke it at any time", "A durable power of attorney names a decision maker", "It requires a provider's approval to be valid", "A DNR order means comfort care is withdrawn"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "An advance directive is the client's voice when they no longer have one, and it is <b>revocable at any time</b> by the client, not the provider. <b>A DNR is not 'do not treat'</b> - pain control, positioning, hygiene and dignity all continue, and that misunderstanding causes real suffering.", "topic": "Oncologic Disorders…", "icon": "🎗️", "topicFull": "Oncologic Disorders & End-of-Life Care", "card": "core/NG-314_neutropenic-precautions-palliative.html", "cardLabel": "Neutropenic Precautions & Palliative Care"}, {"q": "A client near the end of life develops noisy, gurgling respirations. Which nursing action is most appropriate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Perform deep suctioning every hour", "Reposition to a side-lying position and give the prescribed antimuscarinic", "Increase IV fluids to thin the secretions", "Withhold all sedation so the client stays alert"], "ans": [1], "why": "So-called death rattle distresses the <b>family</b> more than the client. Repositioning plus an antimuscarinic such as glycopyrrolate reduces secretions. <b>Deep suctioning is traumatic and ineffective</b>, and extra fluid makes it worse.", "topic": "Oncologic Disorders…", "icon": "🎗️", "topicFull": "Oncologic Disorders & End-of-Life Care", "card": "core/NG-314_neutropenic-precautions-palliative.html", "cardLabel": "Neutropenic Precautions & Palliative Care"}, {"q": "A client with metastatic breast cancer reports new, persistent mid-back pain that started yesterday. She has full strength in both legs and is walking without difficulty. Which action should the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Notify the provider immediately about the new back pain", "Give the ordered acetaminophen and reassess in an hour", "Document the pain and report it if leg weakness develops", "Apply a heating pad and place the client on bed rest"], "ans": [0], "why": "New back pain is the <b>first sign of spinal cord compression</b>, and it appears before any weakness, so reporting it now is what gets steroids and radiation started while she can still walk. Medicating and waiting for weakness is the tempting choice, but by the time weakness or bowel and bladder changes appear the paralysis may be permanent."}, {"q": "A client with lung cancer suddenly has swelling of the face, neck and upper chest with distended neck veins, and reports that his breathing is worse when he lies down. Which nursing action is the priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Raise the head of the bed", "Lay the client flat and elevate both legs", "Apply cool compresses to the face to reduce the swelling", "Restrict oral fluids and recheck the client in one hour"], "ans": [0], "why": "This is <b>superior vena cava syndrome</b>, and because it is worse lying flat, elevating the head of the bed is the immediate priority while radiation is arranged. Laying the client flat with the legs up looks like a reasonable positioning measure but increases upper-body congestion and makes the obstruction and dyspnea worse."}, {"q": "A hospice client with end-stage cancer rates his pain 9 out of 10 despite his scheduled morphine. His daughter asks the nurse not to give any more because it may slow his breathing. What is the nurse's best action?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Titrate the opioid to the client's comfort and explain there is no maximum dose at end of life", "Hold the dose until the client's respiratory rate is above 16", "Give acetaminophen instead so respiratory depression is avoided", "Follow the daughter's wishes and document that the family declined pain medication"], "ans": [0], "why": "At end of life there is <b>no maximum opioid dose</b> when it is titrated to comfort, and the risk of respiratory depression is not a reason to under-treat a dying client's pain. Holding the dose or switching to acetaminophen feels safer but leaves severe pain untreated, and the capable client, not the family, decides his own goals of care."}, {"q": "A client is midway through a course of external beam radiation to the chest. Which statement by the client tells the nurse that more teaching is needed?", "type": "single", "opts": ["\"I dry the treated skin by rubbing it briskly with a towel.\"", "\"I wear a hat and stay out of the sun in the middle of the day.\"", "\"I do not put lotions or ointments on the area unless the provider orders them.\"", "\"My grandchildren can sit on my lap because I am not radioactive.\""], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Skin protection is the priority</b> with external beam radiation, so treated skin is patted dry and never rubbed with a towel. The statement about the grandchildren sounds like a dangerous mistake, but it is correct teaching: external beam clients are not radioactive, and only a client with a brachytherapy source in place emits radiation."}, {"q": "A client is being discharged after placement of permanent brachytherapy seeds. Which instructions should the nurse include? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Sleep in a separate bedroom from your partner", "Flush the toilet several times after each use", "If you find a seed, pick it up with tongs and place it in the lead container", "You may stop straining your urine once you get home", "Share the family bathroom as you normally would"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "With the source in place the client is a radiation source, so home teaching is separate bedrooms and toilets, repeated flushing, and handling a dislodged seed only with <b>tongs into a lead container, never by hand</b>. Stopping the urine strain at discharge is the tempting error, since it must continue <b>7-10 days</b>, and sharing a bathroom repeatedly exposes the family."}, {"q": "A client receiving chemotherapy reports a sore, burning mouth and says he is eating very little because food hurts and tastes strange. Which interventions should the nurse include in the plan of care? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Rinse the mouth with normal saline or a sodium bicarbonate solution", "Apply the ordered topical anesthetic before meals", "Offer cold or room-temperature foods in small, frequent, nutrient-dense portions", "Rinse with an alcohol-based mouthwash after every meal", "Encourage citrus juices and salty broths to stimulate the appetite"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "Saline or bicarbonate rinses, a topical anesthetic before meals, and small frequent cold or room-temperature meals soothe inflamed mucosa and are the best tolerated. An <b>alcohol-based mouthwash</b> is the classic wrong answer because it dries and burns broken mucosa, and acidic or salty items are avoided for the same reason."}], "11": [{"q": "A client with continuous bladder irrigation after a TURP has bright red urine with clots. What does the nurse do first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Decrease the irrigation rate to reduce bladder pressure", "Increase the irrigation rate", "Stop the irrigation and notify the provider", "Deflate the catheter balloon to relieve spasms"], "ans": [1], "why": "Bright red urine with clots means <b>increase the irrigation rate</b>, aiming for a rose colour - CBI does for the prostatic bed what fundal massage does after birth. Slowing or stopping it lets the catheter clot off, and then you are manually irrigating."}, {"q": "Six hours after prostate surgery a client has not voided since catheter removal and reports lower abdominal pressure. What does the nurse do first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Encourage 2 L of fluid over the next 2 hours", "Perform a bladder scan", "Insert a straight catheter", "Run warm water and assist him to stand"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Bladder scan first</b> - it is noninvasive and tells you whether there is urine to drain. Under 50 to 100 mL residual is normal, 100 to 300 is retention and over 300 is significant; pushing fluids before you know only adds volume to a bladder that cannot empty."}, {"q": "A 34-year-old client asks when to perform breast self-examination. Which instruction is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["On the first day of menses each month", "5 to 7 days after menses begins, monthly", "On the same calendar date each month regardless of cycle", "Only at the annual clinical breast exam"], "ans": [1], "why": "BSE is done monthly <b>5 to 7 days after menses starts</b>, when hormonal swelling has settled, and is easiest with soapy skin in the shower using the fingertips. Clinical exams run every 3 years from 20 to 39, then yearly at 40 with annual screening mammography."}, {"q": "On postoperative day 3 after an abdominal hysterectomy the client's temperature has gone 98.6, 99.5, then 101.2 F, the incision is now red and foul-smelling, and pads are saturating every 2 hours. Which actions does the nurse take? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Obtain vital signs and strict intake and output", "Apply warm compresses to the incision", "Administer the prescribed IV antibiotic", "Notify the provider", "Start oral iron supplementation"], "ans": [0, 2, 3], "why": "A rising temperature, a deteriorating incision and accelerating bleeding call for <b>vitals and I&O, IV antibiotics and provider notification</b>. Ambulation and warm compresses are wrong here, and active significant bleeding needs transfusion, not oral iron, which takes weeks."}, {"q": "A nurse is calculating intake and output for a client with continuous bladder irrigation. Which is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Count the irrigant as intake and all drainage as output", "Subtract the irrigant volume so it is not counted in intake or output", "Count only half the irrigant as intake", "Count the irrigant as output only"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>The irrigant is not counted in I&O</b> - it goes in and comes right back out, so including it invents both intake and urine that never existed. Subtract the irrigation volume from total drainage to get true urine output."}, {"q": "A client taking sildenafil for erectile dysfunction should avoid which medication?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Acetaminophen", "Amoxicillin", "Nitroglycerin", "Ibuprofen"], "ans": [2], "why": "PDE5 inhibitors - recognize the <b>-afil</b> ending - are contraindicated with <b>nitrates</b> such as nitroglycerin and amyl nitrate, and cause additive hypotension with doxazosin. Acetaminophen and amoxicillin were specifically named as safe."}, {"q": "A client is being discharged after a transurethral resection of the prostate with continuous bladder irrigation. Which finding requires immediate action?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Pink-tinged urine", "Bladder spasms relieved by the prescribed medication", "No urine output for one hour with lower abdominal distension", "Small clots in the drainage on the first day"], "ans": [2], "why": "No output with a distended abdomen means the catheter is <b>obstructed by clot</b>, and the bladder is filling behind it - this needs irrigation now. Pink urine, spasms and small early clots are all expected. <b>Bright red bleeding with large clots</b> would be the other finding to report.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "renal/NG-052_urinary-retention-bph.html", "cardLabel": "Urinary Retention & BPH"}, {"q": "Which teaching points are correct for a client after a mastectomy with axillary node dissection? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["No blood pressures or venipunctures in the affected arm", "Report redness, warmth or swelling of the arm", "Keep the arm below heart level", "Wear a glove when gardening", "Begin prescribed arm exercises as directed"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 4], "why": "Everything protects an arm that can no longer drain lymph: <b>no cuffs, no needles, no cuts</b>, and any break in the skin gets reported. The arm is <b>elevated</b>, not kept low, because dependency worsens lymphedema.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "skin/NG-318_mastectomy.html", "cardLabel": "Mastectomy & Breast Surgery"}, {"q": "A 17-year-old client arrives in the emergency department with sudden severe left scrotal pain, swelling, and nausea, and the left testicle appears high-riding. Symptoms began 2 hours ago. Which action should the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Notify the provider immediately and prepare the client for surgical evaluation", "Apply ice, elevate the scrotum, and reassess the client in a few hours", "Teach the client to perform a monthly testicular self-exam after a warm shower", "Collect a urine specimen and anticipate treatment for an infection"], "ans": [0], "why": "Sudden severe unilateral pain with swelling, nausea and a high-riding testicle points to <b>testicular torsion</b>, a true surgical emergency with roughly a <b>6-hour window</b> to save the testicle. Waiting and reassessing is the tempting choice but torsion is never a watch-and-see problem, and self-exam teaching or urine testing only delays the surgery that saves the organ."}, {"q": "A 62-year-old client who completed menopause 10 years ago reports a week of vaginal spotting. She has been taking tamoxifen for 3 years. Which response by the nurse is most appropriate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Report the bleeding to the provider today, because bleeding after menopause is never normal", "Explain that occasional light spotting is common in the years after menopause", "Reassure her that spotting is an expected side effect of tamoxifen and needs no follow-up", "Arrange a CA-125 blood test to screen her for a reproductive cancer"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Postmenopausal bleeding is always abnormal and always referred</b> — it is exactly why endometrial cancer tends to be caught earlier than ovarian cancer. Tamoxifen is a reason for higher suspicion, not reassurance, because it raises endometrial cancer risk by stimulating uterine estrogen receptors, and CA-125 tracks known disease and is <b>not a screening test</b>."}, {"q": "A client with benign prostatic hyperplasia is started on tamsulosin and tells the nurse he already gets up two or three times each night to urinate. Which instruction is the priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Sit on the side of the bed for a moment before standing when you get up at night", "Expect this medication to shrink your prostate within about a week", "Take an over-the-counter antihistamine at bedtime to reduce nighttime trips", "Stop taking the medication as soon as your urine stream improves"], "ans": [0], "why": "Tamsulosin relaxes the bladder outlet and causes <b>orthostatic hypotension</b>, which is most dangerous during nighttime voiding, so fall prevention is the priority teaching. Shrinking the prostate is finasteride's effect and takes up to 6 months, and <b>antihistamines worsen retention in BPH</b> rather than helping."}, {"q": "A nurse is caring for a client on the first day after an abdominal hysterectomy. Which finding should the nurse report immediately?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The client has saturated one perineal pad in the past 4 hours", "The indwelling urinary catheter is still in place 18 hours after surgery", "The client is wearing anti-embolism stockings that were applied before surgery", "The client reports cramping abdominal gas pain"], "ans": [0], "why": "The priority postoperative concern after hysterectomy is <b>hemorrhage</b>, and <b>one pad saturated in 4 hours</b> is the stated definition of excessive bleeding. The catheter is the tempting answer, but the Foley is expected to stay in for <b>24 hours</b>, so a catheter still in place at 18 hours is routine, not a reason to call."}, {"q": "A 22-year-old client reports vaginal discharge and pelvic pain that has come and gone for several months and asks to be tested for a sexually transmitted infection. Which nursing actions are appropriate? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Ask how long the symptoms have been going on", "Obtain a cervical swab for testing", "Explain that her sexual partners also need to be treated", "Send a blood sample as the primary test to identify the infection", "Collect a clean-catch urine specimen in place of a cervical swab"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "The workup begins by <b>asking duration</b>, since months of symptoms suggests PID, and the diagnostic specimen is a <b>cervical swab</b>, with partners treated as well. Blood and urine feel like the familiar answers but are not the specimen used for this workup, and a pelvic ultrasound is added only when PID is suspected."}, {"q": "A 41-year-old client comes in for a routine women's health visit and asks what screening she should be having now. Which statements by the nurse are correct? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Avoid douching, vaginal medications and intercourse for 24 hours before your Pap test", "Pap testing is done every 3 years routinely, and more often only if something indicates it", "Now that you are past 40, you should have a screening mammogram every year", "At your age a clinical breast exam is needed only once every 3 years", "A CA-125 blood test should be added to screen you for ovarian cancer"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "Routine Pap screening is <b>every 3 years</b> with no douching, vaginal medications or intercourse for <b>24 hours</b> beforehand, and <b>screening mammography is yearly starting at age 40</b>. The every-3-year clinical breast exam is the trap: that interval applies to ages 20-39 and becomes yearly at 40 and older, and CA-125 tracks known disease rather than screening for it."}], "12": [{"q": "At a mass casualty scene, which client does the nurse tag red and treat first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["An unresponsive client with agonal respirations and massive head trauma", "A client with chest burns, hoarseness and stridor", "A client with an open femur fracture and stable vital signs", "A client walking around with a superficial forearm burn"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Chest burns with hoarseness and stridor are red</b>: life-threatening but survivable with quick intervention. Massive head trauma with agonal respirations is black-tagged, the open femur fracture is yellow, and the ambulatory burn is green."}, {"q": "A client rescued from a house fire has headache and dizziness, and SpO2 reads 98 percent on room air. What does the nurse do?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Document the normal saturation and continue monitoring", "Apply 100 percent oxygen by non-rebreather and obtain a carboxyhemoglobin level", "Apply 2 L by nasal cannula and recheck in an hour", "Prepare for immediate intubation"], "ans": [1], "why": "In carbon monoxide poisoning the <b>pulse oximeter is worthless</b> - the probe cannot distinguish oxyhemoglobin from carboxyhemoglobin, so it reads 98 percent on a poisoned client. Treatment is <b>100 percent oxygen by non-rebreather</b> plus a blood carboxyhemoglobin level; symptoms are mostly neurologic."}, {"q": "A client swallowed a non-corrosive medication 45 minutes ago and is alert with a protected airway. Which intervention does the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Activated charcoal", "Induced vomiting", "Immediate endoscopy", "Supportive care only"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Activated charcoal works for medications within about 1 to 2 hours</b>, since the stomach empties in roughly 2 hours, and requires a protected airway. Corrosives get endoscopy with no charcoal and no lavage, and we never induce vomiting."}, {"q": "Using field triage criteria, which findings assign a red tag? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Respiratory rate of 34", "Capillary refill of 4 seconds", "Respiratory rate of 22", "Unable to follow simple commands", "Ambulatory with a deep laceration to the forearm"], "ans": [0, 1, 3], "why": "The field cutoffs are <b>RR under 10 or over 30, capillary refill over 2 seconds or pulse over 120, and inability to follow commands</b> - blood pressures are not taken, there is no time. RR 22 sits inside the acceptable 10 to 29 range, and a walking client with a laceration is green or yellow."}, {"q": "Three clients are admitted after a suspected anthrax exposure. Which precautions does the nurse implement?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Airborne precautions with negative pressure", "Droplet precautions with a private room", "Standard precautions; anthrax is not spread person to person", "Contact precautions with dedicated equipment"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Anthrax is bacterial and is not transmitted person to person, so no isolation is required</b>; treatment is ciprofloxacin or penicillin. Smallpox (airborne) and plague (droplet) are transmissible - grouping all bioterrorism agents together is the trap."}, {"q": "A client is brought in after a rattlesnake bite to the right forearm. Which action is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Apply a tourniquet above the bite", "Immobilize the limb at or below heart level and remove jewelry", "Apply ice to slow venom absorption", "Elevate the arm above the heart on pillows"], "ans": [1], "why": "Keep her still with the <b>limb at or below heart level, remove jewelry before swelling</b>, and assess edema every 15 to 30 minutes; antivenom is given within 4 to 12 hours. Ice, tourniquets, cutting and elevation are all contraindicated."}, {"type": "sata", "week": "12", "step": "recognize", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty…", "q": "A nurse in the emergency department is assessing a client. Which findings should prompt the nurse to further screen for possible human trafficking? (Select all that apply.)", "opts": ["Multiple bruises and burns in various stages of healing", "The client appears frightened and avoids eye contact while a companion answers all the questions", "Unexplained abdominal pain and a frightened demeanor", "The client arrives with a well-documented, consistent medical history", "Blunt abdominal trauma and periorbital bruising", "The client requests a routine follow-up appointment in 2 weeks"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "Trafficking red flags include bruises, burns, or fractures in various stages of healing, a frightened or agitated demeanor with unexplained abdominal pain or headache, blunt abdominal trauma with black eyes, and a controlling companion who answers all questions for the client. A well-documented, consistent history and a routine follow-up request are unremarkable, reassuring findings that do not raise trafficking concern.", "src": "ATI Adult Med-Surg ch 3 (Emergency Nursing Principles and Management)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing"}, {"q": "During a mass casualty incident using START triage, a client is breathing at 34 breaths per minute with a capillary refill of 4 seconds and cannot follow simple commands. Which category applies?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Green - minor", "Yellow - delayed", "Red - immediate", "Black - expectant"], "ans": [2], "why": "START tags <b>red</b> for a respiratory rate over 30, capillary refill over 2 seconds or absent radial pulse, or inability to follow commands - and this client fails all three. Red means <b>treatable now and will die without it</b>. Black is reserved for those without spontaneous respiration after the airway is opened.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty…", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing"}, {"q": "Which clients would be triaged as green (minor) in a mass casualty event? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["A client with a closed forearm fracture who is walking", "A client with superficial abrasions who is anxious but ambulatory", "A client with a tension pneumothorax", "A client with a sprained ankle who can hop with assistance", "A client in cardiac arrest"], "ans": [0, 1, 3], "why": "Green is the <b>walking wounded</b>: they can move themselves and will survive a wait. A tension pneumothorax is <b>red</b> - it is fixable in seconds with decompression. In a mass casualty, cardiac arrest is <b>black</b>, because resuscitating one person consumes the resources of many. That reversal of everyday priorities is the whole point of disaster triage.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty…", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing"}, {"q": "A client admitted after a motor vehicle crash suddenly becomes severely short of breath. The nurse notes a deviated trachea, absent breath sounds on the right, and unequal chest rise. Which action does the nurse anticipate first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Prepare for immediate needle decompression", "Send the client for a stat chest x-ray to confirm the diagnosis", "Set up for chest tube insertion and wait for the provider", "Place the client in high-Fowler's position and apply a nonrebreather mask"], "ans": [0], "why": "Tension pneumothorax is diagnosed by <b>assessment</b>, not imaging, and needle decompression comes first, with the chest tube after. Waiting for the x-ray or for the chest tube setup delays the one intervention that relieves the pressure now."}, {"q": "A client with a chest tube placed after blunt chest trauma is being repositioned when the tube is accidentally pulled out of the chest wall. Which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Clamp the tube close to the insertion site", "Cover the site with an occlusive dressing taped on three sides", "Apply dry sterile gauze and tape it down on all four sides", "Submerge the end of the tube in sterile water"], "ans": [1], "why": "A tube pulled <b>out</b> of the chest is covered with an occlusive dressing taped on three sides so air can escape but not re-enter. The sterile water seal is the fix for a <b>disconnected</b> system, not a dislodged tube, and clamping is not the answer here."}, {"q": "A client is freed after several hours pinned beneath a collapsed wall. The left leg is swollen but intact and vital signs are currently stable. Which intervention is the priority once the force has been released?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Restrict IV fluids to avoid overloading the kidneys", "Give tetanus prophylaxis before any other intervention", "Begin aggressive IV fluid resuscitation", "Apply a tourniquet proximal to the crushed area"], "ans": [2], "why": "In a crush injury, myoglobin released once the force is lifted causes <b>acute kidney injury</b>, so aggressive fluids are the priority. Restricting fluids is the opposite of what protects the kidneys, and tetanus prophylaxis matters only if the last dose was 5-10 years ago and is not the first action."}, {"q": "A client with a long history of heavy alcohol use arrives intoxicated and drowsy. The nurse has placed her side-lying with a patent airway. Point-of-care glucose is low. Which action does the nurse take next?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Give IV dextrose immediately and follow it with thiamine", "Administer flumazenil to reverse the sedation", "Withhold both until confirmatory laboratory results return", "Administer thiamine before giving glucose"], "ans": [3], "why": "In chronic drinkers, <b>thiamine comes before glucose</b>. Giving the dextrose first feels right because the glucose is low, but the order is the point of the rule; flumazenil is the benzodiazepine antidote and can precipitate seizures."}, {"q": "Several clients arrive at the ED entrance after a chemical release at a nearby plant. Which actions does the nurse take before these clients enter the treatment area? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Bring the most critically injured straight into the ED and decontaminate after stabilization", "Don personal protective equipment before any client contact", "Direct the runoff water into the ambulance bay drain", "Remove all of each client's clothing and jewelry", "Irrigate head-to-toe with copious soap and water in a dedicated decon zone"], "ans": [1, 3, 4], "why": "PPE protects the nurse first, removing clothing and jewelry alone removes most of the contaminant, and copious head-to-toe irrigation happens in a dedicated decon zone. Taking the sickest client in undecontaminated is the tempting <b>everyday</b> priority but it contaminates the ED, and the runoff must be contained."}, {"q": "A hospital-wide disaster alert is announced and the charge nurse must free medical-surgical beds. Which clients does the nurse identify as appropriate for early discharge? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["A client who required needle decompression two hours ago", "A client already awaiting discharge paperwork", "A client whose chest tube water seal is bubbling continuously", "A day-surgery client who is awake and ambulating", "A stable client with a well-controlled chronic condition"], "ans": [1, 3, 4], "why": "The clients discharged first are the <b>most stable</b> - those already awaiting discharge, day-surgery clients, and stable chronic clients. Continuous water-seal bubbling signals an air leak and the post-decompression client is still unstable, so neither is stable enough to leave despite having a plan of care that looks routine."}], "13": [{"q": "A client meets sepsis criteria in the ED. Which action does the nurse perform first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Administer the broad-spectrum antibiotic", "Obtain two sets of blood cultures", "Start a norepinephrine infusion", "Infuse a 30 mL/kg fluid bolus"], "ans": [1], "why": "Order matters within the first hour: labs including lactate, then <b>blood cultures before antibiotics</b>, then antibiotics, then fluids, with vasopressors only if fluids fail. Giving the antibiotic first feels lifesaving but sterilizes the cultures and blinds the whole admission."}, {"q": "A client in cardiogenic shock has troponin I 2.9, BNP 157, crackles, SpO2 88 percent on 4 L and urine output 15 mL/hr. Which intervention improves cardiac output?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A 2 L isotonic fluid bolus", "An inotropic infusion", "Sublingual nitroglycerin", "A unit of packed red blood cells"], "ans": [1], "why": "Cardiogenic shock is pump failure, so the answer is <b>inotropes</b>, not volume - large boluses flood lungs that are already crackling. Nitroglycerin while hypotensive turns an 80 into a 60."}, {"q": "Which set of values indicates septic shock rather than SIRS alone?", "type": "single", "opts": ["BP 110/68, WBC 18,000, lactate 1.8", "BP 78/46, HR 132, lactate 4.2, requiring vasopressors", "Temp 38.5 C, HR 96, RR 22, WBC 13,000", "BP 96/60, HR 104, lactate 1.2"], "ans": [1], "why": "Septic shock requires <b>SBP under 90 and lactate over 2 - with 4 as the key marker - plus a vasopressor requirement</b>. SIRS needs only 2 of 4 criteria and meeting them is not automatically sepsis, which is why the normal-lactate options are distractors."}, {"q": "A client with a C5 spinal cord injury has BP 74/42 and HR 48. Which findings support neurogenic shock? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Bradycardia with hypotension", "Cool clammy skin with capillary refill over 4 seconds", "Warm skin with capillary refill under 2 seconds", "Compensatory tachycardia", "Loss of sympathetic tone below the level of injury"], "ans": [0, 2, 4], "why": "Neurogenic shock is the one shock <b>without tachycardia</b> - the vagus is intact and the sympathetic chain is lost, giving <b>hypotension with bradycardia, warm skin and brisk capillary refill</b>. Treatment is norepinephrine; high-dose corticosteroids are never used in spinal cord injury."}, {"q": "A client has BP 78/50, jugular venous distention, muffled heart sounds and a history of chest trauma. What does the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Inotropic support for cardiogenic shock", "Pericardiocentesis", "Rapid isotonic fluid boluses for hypovolemia", "Needle decompression at the second intercostal space"], "ans": [1], "why": "Beck's triad - <b>muffled heart sounds, JVD and hypotension</b> - is obstructive shock from tamponade, and the definitive answer is <b>pericardiocentesis</b> at the 5th intercostal space, after which BP can jump from 78/50 to 140/80 in minutes. It involves the heart, so it is repeatedly mistaken for cardiogenic shock."}, {"q": "A deteriorating client in shock has PaO2 58, PaCO2 80 and pH 7.31. Which action does the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Increase the nasal cannula to 15 L/min", "Prepare for intubation and mechanical ventilation", "Give sodium bicarbonate", "Begin incentive spirometry every hour"], "ans": [1], "why": "Hypoxemia with a PaCO2 of 80 and falling pH means ventilatory failure, so the answer is <b>intubate</b> - preoxygenate at 100 percent, sedate before paralyzing, and continue sedation after. Cranking a nasal cannula to 15 L/min is not an answer; it cannot fix a ventilation problem."}, {"q": "A client has a blood pressure of 84/50 mm Hg, heart rate 124/min, warm flushed skin, and a temperature of 38.9° C. Which type of shock does the nurse suspect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Hypovolemic", "Cardiogenic", "Septic (distributive)", "Obstructive"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Warm, flushed skin with fever and a wide pulse pressure</b> is early distributive shock from sepsis - the vessels have dilated, so the problem is the container, not the volume or the pump. Hypovolemic and cardiogenic shock both present <b>cold, clammy and vasoconstricted</b>.", "topic": "Shock & MODS", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Shock & MODS", "card": "cardio/NG-050_shock.html", "cardLabel": "Shock"}, {"q": "Which interventions are priorities in the first hour of septic shock? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Obtain blood cultures before antibiotics", "Give broad-spectrum antibiotics", "Begin a 30 mL/kg crystalloid bolus", "Measure a serum lactate", "Start vasopressors before any fluid is given"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 3], "why": "The sepsis bundle is <b>cultures, lactate, antibiotics, fluid</b> - and cultures go first only because drawing them takes moments, never as a reason to delay the antibiotic. <b>Fluid comes before vasopressors</b>: squeezing an empty tank does not perfuse anything.", "topic": "Shock & MODS", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Shock & MODS", "card": "cardio/NG-050_shock.html", "cardLabel": "Shock"}, {"q": "A client in shock has a urine output of 12 mL/hr, a lactate of 4.8 mmol/L, and a mean arterial pressure of 58 mm Hg. What do these findings together indicate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Adequate compensation", "Tissue hypoperfusion requiring escalation", "An expected response to fluid therapy", "Resolving shock"], "ans": [1], "why": "All three say the same thing in different languages: <b>the tissues are not being perfused</b>. A MAP under 65 will not perfuse the kidneys, the urine output proves it, and the lactate is the cells' own report of anaerobic metabolism. Rising lactate is the earliest reliable sign that the treatment is not working.", "topic": "Shock & MODS", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Shock & MODS", "card": "cardio/NG-050_shock.html", "cardLabel": "Shock"}, {"q": "A client admitted with an upper GI hemorrhage has BP 78/50 mm Hg, HR 132/min, RR 28/min, urine output 10 mL/hr, Hgb 8.2 g/dL and Hct 25%. Which intervention does the nurse anticipate first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Rapid isotonic crystalloid, with blood products because bleeding is ongoing", "A norepinephrine infusion titrated to a MAP above 65 mm Hg", "An inotrope to improve cardiac output", "High-flow oxygen by nonrebreather as the primary treatment"], "ans": [0], "why": "A <b>low</b> H&H signals active bleeding, so the treatment is fixing the cause plus fluids, with blood products for the hemorrhage. Norepinephrine is tempting because the MAP is low, but pressors come only <b>after</b> fluids fail, and oxygen alone does not treat the underlying volume loss."}, {"q": "A client receiving an IV antibiotic suddenly develops hives, warm flushed skin, wheezing and BP 82/48 mm Hg. Which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Administer intramuscular epinephrine", "Apply oxygen by nonrebreather mask", "Begin a rapid IV crystalloid bolus", "Elevate the client's legs to shift volume back to the heart"], "ans": [0], "why": "In anaphylactic shock the order is <b>epinephrine first</b>, then oxygen, then fluids. A bolus looks right because the client is hypotensive, but <b>fluids are not the priority</b> here — only epinephrine reverses the histamine release causing the vasodilation."}, {"q": "A client in septic shock is receiving norepinephrine through a peripheral IV while central access is being arranged. The nurse finds the site pale, swollen and cool with the infusion running. Which action does the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Administering phentolamine to reverse the extravasation", "Applying warm compresses and continuing the infusion at that site", "Flushing the line with saline and resuming the norepinephrine", "Increasing the norepinephrine rate to maintain the MAP"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Phentolamine reverses norepinephrine extravasation</b>, and the drug belongs in a central line titrated to MAP. Continuing or restarting the infusion at that site is the trap: norepinephrine causes tissue injury, and prolonged use is also associated with <b>digital necrosis</b>."}, {"q": "A client being treated for shock has BP 86/64 mm Hg with a MAP of 71 mm Hg, HR 96/min, is alert and oriented, and has a urine output of 45 mL/hr. A new nurse wants to bolus fluids for the low systolic pressure. Which response by the charge nurse is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The MAP is above 65, so perfusion is adequate; continue therapy and vital signs every 15 minutes", "Any systolic pressure below 90 requires an immediate fluid bolus", "Start norepinephrine now because the systolic pressure is in the 80s", "Place the client in modified Trendelenburg until the systolic pressure exceeds 90"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>MAP is the perfusion indicator, with a goal above 65</b> — an SBP in the 80s to 90s is not alarming when the MAP is adequate. Bolusing or starting a pressor for the systolic number alone treats a number rather than perfusion, and the urine output above 30 mL/hr and clear mental status confirm the client is perfusing."}, {"q": "A client is 4 hours out from a motor vehicle crash with a suspected splenic injury. Which findings indicate the compensatory stage of shock rather than a later stage? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Heart rate 118/min", "Cool, clammy skin", "Restlessness", "Blood pressure 76/44 mm Hg", "Confusion and disorientation"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "<b>Tachycardia is the earliest response</b>, and restlessness from cerebral hypoxia with cool clammy skin rounds out the compensatory picture while the <b>blood pressure is still normal</b>. A falling BP marks the progressive stage, and confusion or altered mental status is a <b>late</b> sign, not an early one."}, {"q": "A client in progressive shock is being intubated at the bedside. Which actions by the nurse are appropriate? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Preoxygenate with 100% oxygen before the attempt", "Give the sedative before the paralytic agent", "Auscultate for bilateral breath sounds and check for symmetric chest rise after insertion", "Stop sedation once the paralytic has taken effect", "Accept a purple color change on the ETCO2 detector as confirmation of tracheal placement"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "Intubation means <b>preoxygenate at 100%, sedate before you paralyze, and keep sedation going afterward</b>, then confirm with bilateral breath sounds and symmetric chest rise. Stopping sedation leaves a paralyzed client awake, and on ETCO2 <b>purple means esophagus</b> — pull and retry — while yellow or green indicates the trachea, with CXR as the gold standard."}], "14": [{"q": "A nurse receives report on four clients. Which does she assess first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A burn client with facial burns who is now hoarse", "A client with a hemoglobin of 7.4 scheduled for transfusion", "A client with a fasting glucose of 168", "A client with a new prescription for allopurinol"], "ans": [0], "why": "The cross-cutting rule is <b>ABCs first</b>, then urgent over non-urgent, then assess before intervene, then Maslow, then least restrictive. A hoarse voice after facial burns is an airway about to close; the anemia, hyperglycemia and new prescription are all real but none is an airway."}, {"q": "A client is short of breath and also asks for help to the bathroom and reports 6/10 incisional pain. Which priority rule directs the nurse's first action?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Maslow, so the physiologic elimination need comes first", "ABCs, so breathing is addressed first", "Least restrictive, so the client's request is honored first", "Assess before intervene, so pain is rated first"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>ABCs sit above Maslow, above assess-before-intervene and above least restrictive</b> in the hierarchy - breathing is addressed before elimination or pain. The other rules are real, but they only decide the question once airway, breathing and circulation are secure."}, {"q": "A drip is ordered at 5 mcg/kg/min for a client weighing 154 lb. The bag contains 400 mg in 250 mL. What rate does the nurse program?", "type": "single", "opts": ["8.2 mL/hr", "13.1 mL/hr", "21 mL/hr", "26.3 mL/hr"], "ans": [1], "why": "154 divided by 2.2 = 70 kg; 70 x 5 = 350 mcg/min = 21,000 mcg/hr = 21 mg/hr; the bag is 1.6 mg/mL, so 21 divided by 1.6 = <b>13.1 mL/hr</b>. Answering 21 means stopping at mg/hr and never converting to volume - round only at the very end."}, {"q": "Which are steps of the Clinical Judgment Measurement Model? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Recognize cues", "Chart by exception", "Analyze cues", "Prioritize hypotheses", "Delegate to unlicensed personnel"], "ans": [0, 2, 3], "why": "The six steps are <b>recognize cues, analyze cues, prioritize hypotheses, generate solutions, take action and evaluate outcomes</b>, and they head every module review list. Charting and delegation are nursing activities, not steps of the model."}, {"q": "A client's labs return: sodium 138, potassium 5.9, WBC 8,200, hemoglobin 13.4. Which value does the nurse report?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Sodium 138", "Potassium 5.9", "WBC 8,200", "Hemoglobin 13.4"], "ans": [1], "why": "Only the <b>potassium is outside the reference range</b>, and hyperkalemia risks arrhythmia. Values inside normal limits are almost always distractors in select-all and highlight items - the skill being tested is knowing the ranges cold."}, {"q": "With one week left before the cumulative final, which review target gives the highest yield per hour?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Module 13 Shock and MODS", "Module 1 Sensory Disorders", "Module 6 Diabetes", "Module 10 Oncologic Disorders"], "ans": [0], "why": "The final is cumulative, and <b>Module 13 is the only content that has not already appeared on an exam</b>, making it the highest-value target per hour. Note also that the ATI CMS Medical-Surgical Proctored Assessment is scheduled on a separate date from the final - check the calendar, they are days apart."}, {"q": "A nurse receives report on four clients. Which one does the nurse assess first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A client with diabetes whose morning glucose is 244 mg/dL", "A client 1 day post-op reporting incisional pain of 6 out of 10", "A client with a new tracheostomy whose oxygen saturation has fallen from 96% to 88%", "A client with cellulitis awaiting the first dose of antibiotic"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Airway and breathing outrank everything.</b> A falling saturation in a new tracheostomy suggests obstruction or a displaced tube, and that becomes unsurvivable in minutes. The glucose, the pain and the pending antibiotic all matter, and all of them can wait the few minutes this takes.", "topic": "Final Exam Review", "icon": "🎯", "topicFull": "Final Exam Review"}, {"q": "Which findings across body systems require the nurse to act immediately? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Potassium of 6.8 mEq/L", "Widening pulse pressure with bradycardia after a head injury", "Urine output of 12 mL/hr in a burn client", "Hemoglobin of 10.9 g/dL in a client with chronic anemia", "New-onset stridor after extubation"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "Four of these kill quickly: potassium at 6.8 stops the heart, Cushing's triad means herniation is near, oliguria in a burn means failing resuscitation, and stridor is an airway closing. A hemoglobin of 10.9 in someone with <b>chronic</b> anemia is a stable abnormal - which is exactly the distinction these questions are built to test.", "topic": "Final Exam Review", "icon": "🎯", "topicFull": "Final Exam Review"}, {"q": "Fifteen minutes into a unit of packed red blood cells, a client develops chills, flank pain, and a rising temperature. Which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Stop the transfusion and infuse normal saline through new tubing", "Slow the transfusion and continue to monitor the client closely", "Flush the existing tubing with saline and restart the unit", "Leave the line running and notify the provider and blood bank"], "ans": [0], "why": "The module rule for a transfusion reaction is <b>STOP, saline, new tubing</b>. Slowing the rate or flushing the old tubing is tempting because it seems to buy time, but both keep blood reaching the client, which is exactly what must stop first."}, {"q": "Minutes after a new IV medication is started, a client develops lip and tongue swelling, wheezing, and widespread hives. Which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Recheck the blood pressure before acting", "Apply oxygen and raise the head of the bed", "Administer epinephrine IM", "Notify the provider and obtain a full allergy history"], "ans": [2], "why": "For anaphylaxis the module states <b>epinephrine IM first</b>. Rechecking the blood pressure looks right to a student applying assess-before-intervene, but that rule is set aside when it is an emergency with an obvious action."}, {"q": "A client admitted after a head injury was alert on arrival but now needs repeated prompting to answer questions. Vital signs are unchanged from baseline. Which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Document the finding and reassess later in the shift", "Report the change in level of consciousness to the provider", "Recheck the vital signs and act only if they change", "Let the client rest and attribute the change to fatigue"], "ans": [1], "why": "A change in <b>level of consciousness is the earliest sign of rising ICP</b>, so it is reported now. Waiting for the vital signs to change is the trap: unchanged values inside the normal range are reassuring-looking distractors, not evidence that nothing is wrong."}, {"q": "A client is admitted with a markedly elevated blood glucose. Orders include an IV fluid bolus and an insulin infusion. Which does the nurse start first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The IV fluid bolus", "The insulin infusion", "Both at the same time through separate lines", "Neither, until a repeat glucose is drawn"], "ans": [0], "why": "The module rule is <b>fluids before insulin</b>. Starting insulin first is tempting because the glucose is the most obvious abnormality on the chart, but volume is replaced before the glucose is driven down."}, {"q": "A client with extensive burns is receiving Parkland-based fluid resuscitation. Which statements about the nurse's management are correct? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Half of the calculated 24-hour volume is given in the first 8 hours", "Urine output is the gauge of adequate resuscitation", "The client's weight in pounds is divided by 2.2 to obtain kilograms", "The remaining volume is withheld until the second 24-hour period", "A blood pressure within the normal range confirms adequate resuscitation"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "Parkland gives <b>half the total in the first 8 hours</b>, uses weight in kilograms (lb / 2.2), and is judged by <b>urine output</b>. A normal blood pressure is the tempting choice, but values inside the normal reference range are usually distractors and blood pressure falls last."}, {"q": "A client with a spinal cord injury becomes hypotensive and bradycardic. Which statements about the nurse's reasoning are correct? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Bradycardia with hypotension points to neurogenic shock", "A blood pressure still within the normal range would rule out shock", "Blood pressure is a late finding, so earlier cues must be recognized first", "Airway and breathing are addressed before circulation", "A fast rather than slow heart rate would be expected in neurogenic shock"], "ans": [0, 2, 3], "why": "<b>Neurogenic is the bradycardic shock</b>, <b>blood pressure falls last</b>, and priority still runs airway, breathing, then circulation. Expecting tachycardia is the half-knowledge trap carried over from other shock states, and a normal blood pressure never rules shock out."}]};
